01-01","firstIndexDate":"2025-03-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42926261","source":"MED","pmid":"42926261","doi":"10.1007/978-1-0716-5619-8_2","title":"Generation of Functional Gene Knockout Melanoma Cell Lines by CRISPR-Cas9 Gene Editing.","authorString":"Hargadon KM, Bushhouse DZ, Johnson CE, Willams CJ.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Hargadon KM","lastName":"Hargadon","firstName":"Kristian M","initials":"KM","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hargadon Laboratory, Department of Biology, Hampden-Sydney College, Hampden-Sydney, VA, 23943, USA. khargadon@hsc.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Bushhouse DZ","lastName":"Bushhouse","firstName":"David Z","initials":"DZ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hargadon Laboratory, Department of Biology, Hampden-Sydney College, Hampden-Sydney, VA, 23943, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Johnson CE","lastName":"Johnson","firstName":"Coleman E","initials":"CE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hargadon Laboratory, Department of Biology, Hampden-Sydney College, Hampden-Sydney, VA, 23943, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Willams CJ","lastName":"Willams","firstName":"Corey J","initials":"CJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Hargadon Laboratory, Department of Biology, Hampden-Sydney College, Hampden-Sydney, VA, 23943, USA."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"3089","journalIssueId":4298823,"dateOfPublication":"2027 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol","title":"Methods in molecular biology (Clifton, N.J.)","essn":"1940-6029","issn":"1064-3745","nlmid":"9214969","isoabbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-01-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"25-46","abstractText":"Recent advances in the treatment of metastatic melanoma have emerged only from advances in our understanding of melanoma development and progression at the cellular and molecular level. Despite the impact that such advances have made on the clinical management of this cancer over the last decade, additional insights into factors that promote melanoma progression and therapeutic resistance are needed to combat this disease. CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing technology is a powerful tool for studying gene function in a timely and cost-effective manner, enabling the manipulation of specific DNA sequences via a targeted approach. Herein, we describe a protocol for generating functional gene knockouts in melanoma cell lines by CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing, and we present an example application of this protocol for the successful knockout of the Foxc2 transcription factor-encoding gene in the B16-F1 murine melanoma cell line.","affiliation":"Hargadon Laboratory, Department of Biology, Hampden-Sydney College, Hampden-Sydney, VA, 23943, USA. khargadon@hsc.edu.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line, Tumor"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Melanoma","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Forkhead Transcription Factors","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Knockout Techniques","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Melanoma","Transcription factor","Gene knockout","Gene Editing","B16","Foxc2","Crispr-cas9","Limiting Dilution Cloning"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"mesenchyme fork head 1 protein","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"forkhead transcription factors","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-0716-5619-8_2"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-25","firstPublicationDate":"2027-01-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-26","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42668338","source":"MED","pmid":"42668338","doi":"10.1007/7651_2026_714","title":"CRISPR/Cas9 Editing of Human Keratinocytes for Organotypic Modeling of Genetic Epidermal Disorders.","authorString":"Coon A, Ayers JL, Cole C, Tiwaa A, Parihar A, Vincent T, Gudjonsson JE, Sarkar MK, Simpson CL.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Coon A","lastName":"Coon","firstName":"Anthony","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ayers JL","lastName":"Ayers","firstName":"Jessica L","initials":"JL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Cole C","lastName":"Cole","firstName":"Christopher","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Tiwaa A","lastName":"Tiwaa","firstName":"Afua","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Parihar A","lastName":"Parihar","firstName":"Arti","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Vincent T","lastName":"Vincent","firstName":"Thomas","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Gudjonsson JE","lastName":"Gudjonsson","firstName":"Johann E","initials":"JE","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA."},{"affiliation":"Taubman Medical Research Institute, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Sarkar MK","lastName":"Sarkar","firstName":"Mrinal K","initials":"MK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA. mksarkar@med.umich.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Simpson CL","lastName":"Simpson","firstName":"Cory L","initials":"CL","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA. csimp99@uw.edu."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Stem Cell and Regenerative Medicine, University of Washington, Seattle, WA, USA. csimp99@uw.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"3110","journalIssueId":4298419,"dateOfPublication":"2027 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol","title":"Methods in molecular biology (Clifton, N.J.)","essn":"1940-6029","issn":"1064-3745","nlmid":"9214969","isoabbreviation":"Methods Mol Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-01-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"21-40","abstractText":"CRISPR/Cas9-based DNA editing has revolutionized biomedical research by enabling investigators to precisely and permanently inactivate genes in human cells to provide greater insight into the mechanisms that support normal physiology and drive disease pathology. To leverage this technology for cutaneous biology research, we optimized a strategy for efficient gene editing in human keratinocytes, which can be grown into a differentiated organotypic epidermis to extend studies of gene function to a mature 3D tissue model. Here, we describe step-by-step methods for deploying CRISPR/Cas9 to engineer a TERT-immortalized human epidermal keratinocyte line (N/TERT-2G) to generate monoclonal heterozygous and homozygous knockout cell lines. Additionally, we provide a detailed protocol for growing gene-edited keratinocytes into organotypic epidermis, which we have used to model the pathology of monogenic dermatologic disorders that were not replicated in traditional knockout mice.","affiliation":"Department of Dermatology, University of Michigan, Ann Arbor, MI, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Epidermis","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Keratinocytes","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CY","qualifierName":"cytology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Skin Diseases, Genetic","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PA","qualifierName":"pathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Knockout Techniques"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Epidermis","Keratinocytes","Genetic disease","Dna Editing","Crispr/cas9"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/7651_2026_714"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-24","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-24","firstPublicationDate":"2027-01-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1354099","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.10.29.762760","title":"Prevention of  <i>Klebsiella pneumoniae</i>  antibiotic resistance plasmid dissemination by prophylactic or simultaneous CRISPR-Cas9 treatment","authorString":"Morros-Bernaus C, Feakes N, Oko U, Cozens K, van Houte S, Sünderhauf D.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Morros-Bernaus C","lastName":"Morros-Bernaus","firstName":"Clàudia","initials":"C"},{"fullName":"Feakes N","lastName":"Feakes","firstName":"Nia","initials":"N"},{"fullName":"Oko U","lastName":"Oko","firstName":"Urszula","initials":"U","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7839-8330"}},{"fullName":"Cozens K","lastName":"Cozens","firstName":"Keira","initials":"K"},{"fullName":"van Houte S","lastName":"van Houte","firstName":"Stineke","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Sünderhauf D","lastName":"Sünderhauf","firstName":"David","initials":"D"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-7839-8330"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<h4>ABSTRACT</h4>  Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) is one of the most prevailing challenges of the 21  st century, driven in part by pathogens such as  Klebsiella pneumoniae which frequently disseminate plasmids carrying AMR genes within and across species. Mobile CRISPR-Cas9 tools can limit the spread of AMR plasmids through selective targeting but suffer efficacy issues when faced with ecological realism.  Here, we assembled a simple three-strain community to assay the effectivity of mobile CRISPR-Cas9 in preventing dissemination of a clinical  K. pneumoniae bla CTX- M-15 plasmid from its native host. We applied an  Escherichia coli donor carrying a mobile CRISPR-Cas9 plasmid and entirely prevented transfer of the  K. pneumoniae AMR plasmid into a focal, plasmid-free,  E. coli recipient. Under experimental conditions favouring CRISPR-Cas9 plasmid transfer, AMR plasmid uptake was blocked regardless of whether the CRISPR-Cas9 plasmid spread prophylactically or simultaneously with the  K. pneumoniae AMR plasmid.  Our work shows that mobile CRISPR-Cas9 tools can overcome barriers to successfully prevent dissemination of AMR plasmids. Prophylactic application is not essential, and AMR plasmid transfer can even be suppressed when AMR plasmids spread simultaneously with mobile CRISPR-Cas9 tools.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.29.762760"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-30","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-31","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42987985","source":"MED","pmid":"42987985","doi":"10.1016/j.pbiomolbio.2026.101967","title":"AI-enhanced CRISPR-Cas9 therapeutics: Advances in precision, safety, and delivery.","authorString":"Boretti A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Boretti A","lastName":"Boretti","firstName":"Alberto","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Wellington, 6037, New Zealand. Electronic address: a.a.boretti@gmail.com."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"202","journalIssueId":4310985,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Nov","monthOfPublication":11,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Prog Biophys Mol Biol","title":"Progress in biophysics and molecular biology","essn":"1873-1732","issn":"0079-6107","nlmid":"0401233","isoabbreviation":"Prog Biophys Mol Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-11-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"101967","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing has transformed biomedical research and is advancing toward clinical translation. The integration of artificial intelligence (AI) offers improved precision in guide RNA design, off-target prediction, and delivery optimization. This review synthesizes recent advances in CRISPR-Cas9 for medical applications, covering core mechanisms, engineered variants, base and prime editors, and therapeutic applications including CAR T-cell and CAR-NK engineering, genetic disorders, infectious diseases, and regenerative medicine. Delivery strategies are evaluated with emphasis on AI-driven optimization. AI applications are highlighted across deep learning architectures for guide RNA design, AI-designed nucleases such as OpenCRISPR-1, structure-guided and AI-assisted design of compact high-fidelity SaCas9 variants, reinforcement learning for pipeline optimization, and roles in CAR T-cell therapy and safety assessment. Safety challenges are examined, including off-target effects, chromosomal rearrangements, immunogenicity, and regulatory concerns. Future directions emphasize multi-scale modeling, explainable AI, clinical validation, and integration with synthetic biology, single-cell sequencing, organoids, and nanomaterials. The convergence of AI and CRISPR-Cas9 holds substantial potential for personalized medicine.","affiliation":"Wellington, 6037, New Zealand. Electronic address: a.a.boretti@gmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Artificial intelligence","Genome Editing","Crispr-cas9","Off-target Prediction","Car T-cell Therapy","Base Editing","Guide Rna Design","Prime Editing"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pbiomolbio.2026.101967"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-07","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-11-05","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-05","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-07","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42808239","source":"MED","pmid":"42808239","doi":"10.1080/15257770.2026.2740083","title":"\"The pharmacological potential of CRISPR-Cas9: Revolutionizing gene therapy and drug development\".","authorString":"Acharyya S, Behera B, Pradhan R, Debata J, Patra PK, Giri S, Kumar HK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Acharyya S","lastName":"Acharyya","firstName":"Suman","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4059-9364"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose Institute of Pharmacy, Nadia, West Bengal, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Behera B","lastName":"Behera","firstName":"Bipasha","initials":"B","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6417-9984"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Institute of Pharmacy and Technology Salipur, Cuttack, Odisha, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Pradhan R","lastName":"Pradhan","firstName":"Ritika","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4844-2931"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Institute of Pharmacy and Technology Salipur, Cuttack, Odisha, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Debata J","lastName":"Debata","firstName":"Jitendra","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3142-5362"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Bengal College of Pharmaceutical Technology, Birbhum, West Bengal, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Patra PK","lastName":"Patra","firstName":"Pratap Kumar","initials":"PK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1074-5749"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, School of Pharmacy & Life Sciences, Centurion University of Technology & Management, Bhubaneswar, Odisha, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Giri S","lastName":"Giri","firstName":"Soubhanik","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-2951-9126"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose Institute of Pharmacy, Nadia, West Bengal, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar HK","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"H K","initials":"HK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9755-3052"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Institute of Pharmacy and Technology Salipur, Cuttack, Odisha, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6417-9984"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1074-5749"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9755-3052"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3142-5362"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4059-9364"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0005-2951-9126"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-4844-2931"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4258124,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nucleosides Nucleotides Nucleic Acids","title":"Nucleosides, nucleotides & nucleic acids","essn":"1532-2335","issn":"1525-7770","nlmid":"100892832","isoabbreviation":"Nucleosides Nucleotides Nucleic Acids"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1-33","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9 has entered the mainstream of genetic research and therapeutic development, offering a level of programmable and targeted gene editing that has substantially expanded the possibilities of genetic manipulation. This review focuses on the discovery to development of CRISPR-Cas9 and its recent practice in pharmacological and biotechnology field. We discuss further advancements in CRISPR technology such as high-fidelity CRISPR and new systems Cas12 and Cas13 which increases efficiency of editing and opens more opportunities. CRISPR technology has been further advanced when combined with Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning; guide RNA is now optimized while the off-target effects are predicted. The review also focuses on the new targets for therapy, recent trials, the legal and the social and ethical relevance of the CRISPR approach. With the development of CRISPR-Cas9 promising and revolutionary preliminary achievements in the fields of drug development and disease management and tissue engineering are anticipated. Although, in its application, there are questions that have been raised concerning side effects, carriers, and ethics, it is important to take advantage of it in the right manner. As seen in this review, CRISPR-Cas9 plays the crucial part of the development of biomedical studies and therapeutic paradigms.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy, Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose Institute of Pharmacy, Nadia, West Bengal, India.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Pharmacology","Regenerative Medicine","Gene Editing","Crispr-cas9","Therapeutic Development","Ai Integration"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/15257770.2026.2740083"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-29","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42798792","source":"MED","pmid":"42798792","pmcid":"PMC13613094","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13613094"]},"doi":"10.1002/hsr2.73308","title":"CRISPR-Cas9 in Cancer Therapy: Overcoming Delivery Barriers and Off-Target Effects for Clinical Translation.","authorString":"Mohiuddin M, Ahsan MAM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mohiuddin M","lastName":"Mohiuddin","firstName":"Md","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7313-8655"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy Southeast University Dhaka Bangladesh."}]}},{"fullName":"Ahsan MAM","lastName":"Ahsan","firstName":"Md Al Mamun","initials":"MAM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-0955-6934"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Public Health Daffodil International University Dhaka Bangladesh."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7313-8655"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-0955-6934"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"9","journalIssueId":4270192,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Health Sci Rep","title":"Health science reports","essn":"2398-8835","issn":"2398-8835","nlmid":"101728855","isoabbreviation":"Health Sci Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"e73308","abstractText":"<h4>Background and aims</h4>CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing has transformed molecular biology and has great potential as a therapeutic tool for cancer and many other human diseases. This perspective review elaborates on the recent advances in CRISPR-Cas9 technology and its application as an innovative cancer treatment. Additionally, this perspective outlines the current barriers to clinical translation, including <i>in vivo</i> delivery and off-target effects, as well as potential mitigation strategies.<h4>Discussion</h4>CRISPR-Cas9 has enabled the development of innovative methods to enhance immunotherapy, overcome drug resistance, and identify new therapeutic targets. Importantly, CRISPR-Cas9-induced DNA double-strand breaks can activate p53-mediated selection and cause unintended on-target genomic alterations, including large deletions, chromosomal rearrangements, and chromosome loss. While progress has been made toward addressing these significant obstacles, optimizing delivery systems, minimizing off-target effects, and addressing immunogenicity are critical for the successful clinical application of CRISPR-Cas9. Emerging strategies, including high-fidelity Cas9 variants, optimized guide RNAs, and advanced non-viral delivery systems such as lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) and exosomes, may improve the precision, safety, and delivery of CRISPR-Cas9-based therapies. Continued innovation in base editing and prime editing may enable the development of more refined approaches for precise genome modification.<h4>Conclusion</h4>From a translational perspective, we argue that improvements in editing efficiency alone will be insufficient; clinical success will require the simultaneous achievement of tumor-selective delivery, genomic integrity, and acceptable long-term safety. Therefore, the continued development of novel approaches should be promoted to realize the full potential of CRISPR-Cas9 technology in oncology and other therapeutic applications.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy Southeast University Dhaka Bangladesh.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["article-commentary","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cancer therapy","Gene Editing","In Vivo Delivery","Off‐target Effects","Crispr‐cas9","Immunotherapy Enhancement"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/hsr2.73308"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13613094"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13613094?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-26","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-26","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-28","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-26","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42720610","source":"MED","pmid":"42720610","doi":"10.1080/17435889.2026.2732014","title":"CRISPR-Cas9‑based lipid nanocarriers for advanced therapy of urinary bladder cancer.","authorString":"Mukherjee S, Karati D, Sarkar S, Ash D, Ganguly SC, Dutta A, Kesarla R.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mukherjee S","lastName":"Mukherjee","firstName":"Swarupananda","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6511-3116"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Bhawanipur Global Campus, Kolkata, India."},{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Technology, NSHM Knowledge Campus, Kolkata - Group of Institutions, Kolkata, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Karati D","lastName":"Karati","firstName":"Dipanjan","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Technology, School of Pharmacy, Techno India University, Kolkata, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Sarkar S","lastName":"Sarkar","firstName":"Sinjini","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Technology, Shobhaben Pratapbhai Patel School of Pharmacy and Technology Management, SVKM's Narsee Monjee Institute of Management Studies, Mumbai, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ash D","lastName":"Ash","firstName":"Dipanjana","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Technology, BCDA College of Pharmacy and Technology, Kolkata, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ganguly SC","lastName":"Ganguly","firstName":"Shayeri Chatterjee","initials":"SC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Deprtment of Pharmaceutical Technology, Brainware University, Kolkata, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Dutta A","lastName":"Dutta","firstName":"Ayon","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Deprtment of Pharmaceutical Technology, Brainware University, Kolkata, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kesarla R","lastName":"Kesarla","firstName":"Rajesh","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Technology, Parul Institute of Pharmacy, Parul University, Vadodara, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6511-3116"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"18","volume":"21","journalIssueId":4275348,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Nanomedicine (Lond)","title":"Nanomedicine (London, England)","essn":"1748-6963","issn":"1743-5889","nlmid":"101278111","isoabbreviation":"Nanomedicine (Lond)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"2695-2705","abstractText":"Bladder cancer (BCa) exhibits significant genetic and phenotypic variability. This variability suggests that various tumor subtypes could be influenced by several biomarkers and signaling pathways, which presents a problem for monotherapy strategies. Despite the initial effectiveness of traditional therapies, BCa's high rates of progression and recurrence, and the eventual development of drug resistance in many patients, continue to be major concerns. Because of the potential to transform the genetic modifications linked to the disease, genome editing using CRISPR/Cas9 has become a transformative tool in medicine with noteworthy potential for BCa therapy. Although the CRISPR/Cas9 technology is incredibly effective at transforming the field of genome editing, its instability and cellular impermeability pose significant challenges to its delivery. To increase efficient delivery of CRISPR/Cas9, nanovectors may be investigated. Significant promise exists for improving the therapeutic potential of CRISPR-Cas9 technology and addressing complex cancer therapy difficulties because of the rapid development of nanotechnology-based delivery systems. Relevant articles were searched in Google Scholar, Scopus, and Web of Science covering studies published between 2007 and 2026. Along with the impact of lipid-based nanoparticles on their safe transport to cancer cells, this review emphasizes the significance of the CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing system in the treatment of BCa.","affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Bhawanipur Global Campus, Kolkata, India.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Lipids","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Drug Carriers","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Urinary Bladder Neoplasms","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"DT","qualifierName":"drug therapy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nanoparticles","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genetic Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"