, KL College of Pharmacy, Koneru Lakshmaiah Education Foundation, Vaddeswaram, Guntur, Andhra Pradesh, 522302, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ranjan R","lastName":"Ranjan","firstName":"Rajeev","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0183-1208"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, ISF College of Pharmacy, Ghal Kalan, Moga, Punjab, 142001, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar V","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Vikas","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-8536-4398"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, ISF College of Pharmacy, Ghal Kalan, Moga, Punjab, 142001, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Raj Pal R","lastName":"Raj Pal","firstName":"Ravi","initials":"R","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6798-5009"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, ISF College of Pharmacy, Ghal Kalan, Moga, Punjab, 142001, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6798-5009"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7675-6523"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4710-6350"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-8501-4120"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-5123-6240"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0003-8536-4398"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-9264-9788"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0008-0183-1208"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4247730,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Pharm Des","title":"Current pharmaceutical design","essn":"1873-4286","issn":"1381-6128","nlmid":"9602487","isoabbreviation":"Curr Pharm Des"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Gene editing has enormous potential in biomedical fields, including cancer and personalized medicine. CRISPR-Cas9 is a gene-editing system in which the Cas9 enzyme, guided by RNA derived from short palindromic repeats, alters DNA sequences to inhibit oncogenes through base and prime editing, thereby suppressing tumor growth. Despite significant advancements in anticancer therapies, limitations such as off-target effects, ethical concerns, and challenges in targeted delivery restrict its potential clinical applications. In the present review, we explore the mechanisms of CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing, recent technological advancements, and prospects for cancer management. We also provide insights into strategies for precise delivery, improved targeting accuracy, and the regulatory considerations surrounding CRISPR-Cas9 for oncological applications. Additionally, this review examines the potential of CRISPR-Cas9 in personalized cancer therapy and discusses approaches to enhance tumor-specific targeting and facilitate clinical translation.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, Geeta Institute of Pharmacy, Geeta University, Panipat, Haryana, 132145, India.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Immunotherapy","Targeted Therapy","Cancer Treatment","Gene Editing","Oncology Innovations"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2174/0113816128470361260803050708"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-31","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-19","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-19","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42891947","source":"MED","pmid":"42891947","pmcid":"PMC13697262","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13697262"]},"doi":"10.1016/j.xpro.2026.104904","title":"Protocol for eVLP-mediated CRISPR-Cas9 and prime editing in pancreatic cancer cell lines.","authorString":"Timmins-Young C, Cordani M, Giovannetti E, Aslanoglou D, Gutierrez A, Costello E, Glenn M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Timmins-Young C","lastName":"Timmins-Young","firstName":"Chloe","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liverpool Experimental Cancer Medicine Centre, University of Liverpool, Liverpool L69 3GE, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Cordani M","lastName":"Cordani","firstName":"Marco","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9342-4862"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Faculty of Biological Sciences, Complutense University of Madrid, Madrid, Spain; San Carlos Health Research Institute (IdISSC), 28040 Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Giovannetti E","lastName":"Giovannetti","firstName":"Elisa","initials":"E","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7565-7504"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Medical Oncology, Cancer Center Amsterdam, Amsterdam University Medical Center, Vrije Universiteit, Amsterdam, the Netherlands; Cancer Pharmacology Lab, Fondazione Pisana per La Scienza, Pisa, San Giuliano Terme, Italy."}]}},{"fullName":"Aslanoglou D","lastName":"Aslanoglou","firstName":"Despoina","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Medicine, Pharmacy and Biomedical Sciences, University of Portsmouth, Portsmouth, UK."}]}},{"fullName":"Gutierrez A","lastName":"Gutierrez","firstName":"Alvaro","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Faculty of Pharmacy, Complutense University of Madrid, Madrid, Spain; San Carlos Health Research Institute (IdISSC), 28040 Madrid, Spain."}]}},{"fullName":"Costello E","lastName":"Costello","firstName":"Eithne","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liverpool Experimental Cancer Medicine Centre, University of Liverpool, Liverpool L69 3GE, UK. Electronic address: ecostell@liverpool.ac.uk."}]}},{"fullName":"Glenn M","lastName":"Glenn","firstName":"Mark","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liverpool Experimental Cancer Medicine Centre, University of Liverpool, Liverpool L69 3GE, UK. Electronic address: maglenn@liverpool.ac.uk."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9342-4862"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7565-7504"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"4","volume":"7","journalIssueId":4275788,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"STAR Protoc","title":"STAR protocols","essn":"2666-1667","issn":"2666-1667","nlmid":"101769501","isoabbreviation":"STAR Protoc"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"104904","abstractText":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) cell lines resist transfection, limiting gene-editing experiments. Here, we present a protocol for delivering CRISPR-Cas9 or prime editors into PDAC cell lines using retrovirus-derived engineered virus-like particles (eVLPs). We describe steps for generating eVLPs, packaging CRISPR-Cas9 ribonucleoproteins or prime editors with engineered prime-editing guide RNAs (epegRNAs), and transducing human PDAC cells. We use the HEK3 locus and NQO1 (NAD(P)H quinone dehydrogenase 1) exon 6 as example edits and controls, respectively. This protocol adapts readily to other genomic sites.","affiliation":"Liverpool Experimental Cancer Medicine Centre, University of Liverpool, Liverpool L69 3GE, UK.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cancer","Crispr","Biotechnology And Bioengineering"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2026.104904"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13697262"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13697262?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["ensembl","doi"]},"dateOfCreation":"2026-10-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-28","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-19","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-27","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42983612","source":"MED","pmid":"42983612","doi":"10.2174/0115665232433217251212091333","title":"Nanoparticle-Mediated Delivery of CRISPR/Cas9 Rnps Into Cells.","authorString":"Rajeshwar V, Kumar KR, Kumar GR, Roja P, Mohan VK, Aparna TN.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Rajeshwar V","lastName":"Rajeshwar","firstName":"V","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4251-5550"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, School of Pharmacy, Anurag University, Hyderabad - 500088, Telangana, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar KR","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Konda Ravi","initials":"KR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4793-1630"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, Hetero Institute of Pharmaceutical Sciences, B. Gangaram, Sathupally - 507303, Telangana, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar GR","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Gollapalli Rajeev","initials":"GR","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8686-5800"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacy Practice, School of Pharmacy, Anurag University, Hyderabad, Telangana, 500088, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Roja P","lastName":"Roja","firstName":"Pathakota","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2888-1626"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacology, Sree Dattha Institute of Pharmacy, Hyderabad - 501510, Telangana, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Mohan VK","lastName":"Mohan","firstName":"Vasanth Kumar","initials":"VK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2482-8916"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmacognosy, School of Pharmacy, Takshashila University, Ongur, Tindivanam- 604305, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Aparna TN","lastName":"Aparna","firstName":"T Naga","initials":"TN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, G. Pulla Reddy College of Pharmacy, Mehdipatnam, Hyderabad, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2482-8916"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2888-1626"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4251-5550"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8686-5800"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-4793-1630"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4291238,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Gene Ther","title":"Current gene therapy","essn":"1875-5631","issn":"1566-5232","nlmid":"101125446","isoabbreviation":"Curr Gene Ther"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"The clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)/CRISPRassociated protein 9 (Cas9) system has revolutionized genome editing through its precision, simplicity, and programmability. The clinical translation of CRISPR/Cas9 Ribonucleoproteins (RNPs) remains constrained by major delivery challenges, including large molecular size, instability in biological fluids, and limited cellular penetration. Nanoparticle-mediated, non-viral delivery systems have emerged as a compelling strategy to overcome these barriers by providing targeted, transient, and biocompatible transport of Cas9 RNPs into cells. This review systematically evaluates recent advances in lipid-based, polymeric, peptide, and exosome-derived nanocarriers, focusing on their design principles, mechanisms of uptake, and endosomal escape strategies. Particular attention is given to receptor-mediated targeting, intracellular trafficking, and the emerging translation of lipid nanoparticle systems such as NTLA-2001, which exemplify clinical progress in genome editing. Despite these advancements, several challenges persist. Nanoparticle systems exhibit trade-offs between efficiency, biocompatibility, and scalability. For example, lipid nanoparticles may trigger anti- PEG immune reactions, exosome production faces manufacturing constraints, and polymeric systems risk cytotoxicity or limited degradability. Pre-existing immunity to Cas9 proteins and unpredictable immune responses to nanoparticle components remain significant translational hurdles. By integrating current preclinical and early clinical evidence, this review highlights both the promise and the limitations of nanoparticle-mediated CRISPR/Cas9 RNP delivery. Understanding these biological, immunological, and engineering challenges will be crucial for developing safe, efficient, and clinically scalable genome editing platforms.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutics, School of Pharmacy, Anurag University, Hyderabad - 500088, Telangana, India.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Targeted Therapy","Gene Editing","Nanoparticle Delivery","Endosomal Escape","Crispr/cas9","Cas9 Rnp"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2174/0115665232433217251212091333"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-05","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-29","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-06","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42849713","source":"MED","pmid":"42849713","doi":"10.1016/j.oftale.2026.502657","title":"Advances in precision medicine for the anterior segment: therapeutic applications using CRISPR-Cas9.","authorString":"León Agudelo JA, León Agudelo DF, Martínez Morales MC, Vásquez Villamil VV, Pacheco Ricardo JD, Enríquez Medina MP.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"León Agudelo JA","lastName":"León Agudelo","firstName":"J A","initials":"JA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia. Electronic address: julian_leon93@hotmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"León Agudelo DF","lastName":"León Agudelo","firstName":"D F","initials":"DF","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia."}]}},{"fullName":"Martínez Morales MC","lastName":"Martínez Morales","firstName":"M C","initials":"MC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia."}]}},{"fullName":"Vásquez Villamil VV","lastName":"Vásquez Villamil","firstName":"V V","initials":"VV","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia."}]}},{"fullName":"Pacheco Ricardo JD","lastName":"Pacheco Ricardo","firstName":"J D","initials":"JD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia."}]}},{"fullName":"Enríquez Medina MP","lastName":"Enríquez Medina","firstName":"M P","initials":"MP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0000-2801-7988"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4286384,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Arch Soc Esp Oftalmol (Engl Ed)","title":"Archivos de la Sociedad Espanola de Oftalmologia","essn":"2173-5794","issn":"2173-5794","nlmid":"101715860","isoabbreviation":"Arch Soc Esp Oftalmol (Engl Ed)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"502657","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9 gene editing has emerged as one of the most promising strategies in precision medicine applied to ophthalmology. Although its initial development focused on inherited retinal diseases, recent advances have increasingly targeted anterior segment disorders, including herpetic keratitis, hereditary corneal dystrophies, and genetically associated glaucoma. The ability to modify specific DNA or RNA sequences enables direct intervention in molecular pathways involved in inflammatory, degenerative, and infectious disease progression. This review summarizes the biological basis of CRISPR-Cas systems and discusses the main preclinical and translational findings related to corneal and glaucomatous diseases. Current limitations, including off-target effects, immunogenicity, and challenges associated with molecular delivery into ocular tissues, are also addressed. Available evidence suggests that CRISPR-Cas9 may significantly transform the future management of multiple anterior segment diseases.","affiliation":"Servicio de Oftalmología, Hospital Regional Alfonso Jaramillo Salazar, Líbano, Tolima, Colombia. Electronic address: julian_leon93@hotmail.com.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cornea","glaucoma","Herpetic Keratitis","Anterior Segment","Gene Editing","Córnea","Crispr-cas9","Queratitis Herpética","Segmento Anterior","Edición Génica"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oftale.2026.502657"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-23","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-08","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-08","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42754893","source":"MED","pmid":"42754893","pmcid":"PMC13587479","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13587479"]},"doi":"10.1186/s13073-026-01772-1","title":"Accurate characterization of CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing outcomes and mosaicism with near-perfect long reads.","authorString":"Höijer I, van Schendel R, Emmanouilidou A, Östlund R, Bunikis I, Tijsterman M, den Hoed M, Ameur A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Höijer I","lastName":"Höijer","firstName":"Ida","initials":"I","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3915-3384"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden. ida.hoijer@igp.uu.se."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden. ida.hoijer@igp.uu.se."}]}},{"fullName":"van Schendel R","lastName":"van Schendel","firstName":"Robin","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Human Genetics, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, The Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"Emmanouilidou A","lastName":"Emmanouilidou","firstName":"Anastasia","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Östlund R","lastName":"Östlund","firstName":"Rebecka","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Bunikis I","lastName":"Bunikis","firstName":"Ignas","initials":"I","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Tijsterman M","lastName":"Tijsterman","firstName":"Marcel","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Human Genetics, Leiden University Medical Center, Leiden, The Netherlands."}]}},{"fullName":"den Hoed M","lastName":"den Hoed","firstName":"Marcel","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden."}]}},{"fullName":"Ameur A","lastName":"Ameur","firstName":"Adam","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6085-6749"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden. adam.ameur@igp.uu.se."},{"affiliation":"SciLifeLab, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden. adam.ameur@igp.uu.se."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6085-6749"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-3915-3384"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4259383,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Genome Med","title":"Genome medicine","essn":"1756-994X","issn":"1756-994X","nlmid":"101475844","isoabbreviation":"Genome Med"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"135","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Genetic mosaicism is a well-recognized consequence of CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing, yet its characterization remains challenging, especially when it involves low-frequency structural variants. A comprehensive analysis of mosaicism requires deep and unbiased sequencing of the target loci, with accurate single-molecule reads.<h4>Methods</h4>We performed amplification-free PureTarget PacBio sequencing to investigate CRISPR-Cas9 outcomes at on-target and off-target sites in genome edited zebrafish and their offspring. CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing was performed by micro-injection in fertilized eggs at the single-cell stage.<h4>Results</h4>Thirty samples from pooled larvae and individual zebrafish were successfully sequenced, resulting in > 1100x average target coverage. The PacBio reads reached an exceptional accuracy (QV39) over the target regions, with every read originating from a unique DNA molecule. The two haplotypes of the target loci displayed a balanced depth of coverage, while long-range PCR of the same samples resulted in skewed data. Further analysis of the PureTarget data revealed widespread genetic mosaicism in individual founder (F0) fish, with up to 18 distinct on-target events and 11 off-target events present in a single adult founder. Several CRISPR-Cas9 editing outcomes, including large structural variants and off-target mutations, were inherited to the F1 generation. Notably, as many as seven unique editing events were found among sibling F1 juvenile offspring derived from a single founder pair, thereby confirming the presence of genetic mosaicism in germ cells of founder zebrafish. This implies that some consequences of CRISPR-Cas9 editing may emerge only in the second generation. We also analyzed DNA methylation signals in the PureTarget data but did not observe altered 5mC CpG levels in genome edited samples.<h4>Conclusions</h4>PureTarget enables efficient, accurate, and unbiased profiling of genetic mosaicism and DNA methylation at pre-defined genomic regions. Our results show that CRISPR-Cas9-induced mosaicism is widespread and represents an important factor to consider in genome editing experiments.","affiliation":"Department of Immunology, Genetics and Pathology, Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden. ida.hoijer@igp.uu.se.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Uppsala University","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Zebrafish","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mosaicism"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Genome"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"High-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Mosaicism","Structural Variation","Genome Editing","Single-molecule Sequencing","Targeted Sequencing","Crispr-cas9","Off-target Mutations","Long-read Sequencing","Puretarget"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13073-026-01772-1"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13587479"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13587479?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"PPR","id":"PPR1080897","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["bioproject"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-18","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-20","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-18","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-20","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1355731","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.10.30.763197","title":"Cell-in-drop genotyping detects megabase-scale loss of heterozygosity as a quantitative marker for CRISPR-Cas9 on-target toxicity","authorString":"Medhi D, Mazutis L, Jasin M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Medhi D","lastName":"Medhi","firstName":"Darpan","initials":"D"},{"fullName":"Mazutis L","lastName":"Mazutis","firstName":"Linas","initials":"L"},{"fullName":"Jasin M","lastName":"Jasin","firstName":"Maria","initials":"M"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Targeted DNA double strand breaks catalyzed by CRISPR-Cas9 have been demonstrated to cause megabase-scale segmental deletions and whole chromosome losses in a wide variety of mouse and human cell lines. This raises safety concerns for CRISPR-Cas9 based therapeutics and highlights the need for scalable methods to assess its on-target genotoxicity in clinical contexts. In this report, we present cell-in-drop genotyping, as a high throughput approach that combines single cell encapsulation with droplet-based PCR genotyping of endogenous polymorphisms to detect loss of heterozygosity (LOH) at a targeted chromosome without any genome manipulation. With this approach, we detected megabase scale LOH induced by Cas9 DSBs, mostly frequently by segmental loss. This approach also detected LOH from loss of a critical DNA repair factor, BLM helicase, and LOH induced by other genotoxic conditions, in particular, the chemotherapeutic drugs Paclitaxel and Mitomycin C, which were demonstrated to induce LOH by different mechanisms. A modified version of the assay also captured rare local short range LOH arising from DSB-induced interhomologue recombination. Because it requires no genome manipulation, cell-in-drop genotyping offers a practical, scalable tool for evaluating genotoxic risk of CRISPR-Cas9 and other agents in research and clinical development.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.30.763197"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-11-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42560391","source":"MED","pmid":"42560391","doi":"10.1007/s00425-026-05115-7","title":"In vitro CRISPR/Cas9-RNP cleavage of CcYUC1 in Coffea canephora.","authorString":"Uc-Chuc MA, Aguilar-Hernández V, Jiménez-Ramírez IA, Brito-Argaez L, Loyola-Vargas VM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Uc-Chuc MA","lastName":"Uc-Chuc","firstName":"Miguel A","initials":"MA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8199-7735"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratorio de Biología Celular, Centro de Investigaciones Regionales \"Dr. Hideyo Noguchi\", Universidad Autónoma de Yucatán, Av. Itzáes, No. 490 X Calle 59, Col. Centro, CP 97000, Mérida, Yucatán, México. ma.uc@outlook.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Aguilar-Hernández V","lastName":"Aguilar-Hernández","firstName":"Víctor","initials":"V","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7308-4047"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unidad de Biología Integrativa, Centro de Investigación Científica de Yucatán, Calle 43, No. 130, Chuburná de Hidalgo, CP 97205, Mérida, Yucatán, México."}]}},{"fullName":"Jiménez-Ramírez IA","lastName":"Jiménez-Ramírez","firstName":"Irma A","initials":"IA","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8318-1980"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unidad de Biología Integrativa, Centro de Investigación Científica de Yucatán, Calle 43, No. 130, Chuburná de Hidalgo, CP 97205, Mérida, Yucatán, México."}]}},{"fullName":"Brito-Argaez L","lastName":"Brito-Argaez","firstName":"Ligia","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4742-6813"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unidad de Biología Integrativa, Centro de Investigación Científica de Yucatán, Calle 43, No. 130, Chuburná de Hidalgo, CP 97205, Mérida, Yucatán, México."}]}},{"fullName":"Loyola-Vargas VM","lastName":"Loyola-Vargas","firstName":"Víctor M","initials":"VM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5386-4265"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Unidad de Biología Integrativa, Centro de Investigación Científica de Yucatán, Calle 43, No. 130, Chuburná de Hidalgo, CP 97205, Mérida, Yucatán, México. vmloyola@cicy.mx."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5386-4265"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7308-4047"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8199-7735"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-4742-6813"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-8318-1980"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"3","volume":"264","journalIssueId":4236397,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Planta","title":"Planta","essn":"1432-2048","issn":"0032-0935","nlmid":"1250576","isoabbreviation":"Planta"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"86","abstractText":"<h4>Main conclusion</h4>An in vitro CRISPR/Cas9-RNP system efficiently cleaves CcYUC1 in Coffea canephora, establishing a foundation for DNA-free genome editing in coffee. Somatic embryogenesis depends on auxin biosynthesis and signaling; however, functional validation of candidate genes remains limited. In this study, we identified CcYUC1, a putative flavin monooxygenase gene associated with indole-3-acetic acid biosynthesis, during SE induction in Coffea canephora. CcYUC1 transcripts accumulated during the early stages of SE, suggesting a role in embryogenic induction. To establish a genome editing platform in coffee, we designed a CRISPR/Cas9 ribonucleoprotein (RNP) system targeting exon 4 of CcYUC1. In vitro cleavage assays confirmed specific and efficient digestion of the target, achieving over 80% cleavage under optimized Cas9/sgRNA conditions. These findings establish a proof-of-concept CRISPR/Cas9-RNP platform for DNA-free genome editing in coffee and provide a basis for future functional studies of auxin biosynthesis during somatic embryogenesis.","affiliation":"Laboratorio de Biología Celular, Centro de Investigaciones Regionales \"Dr. Hideyo Noguchi\", Universidad Autónoma de Yucatán, Av. Itzáes, No. 490 X Calle 59, Col. Centro, CP 97000, Mérida, Yucatán, México. ma.uc@outlook.com.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Secretaría de Ciencia, Humanidades, Tecnología e Innovación (Secihti)","grantId":"1515","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Secretaría de Ciencia, Humanidades, Tecnología e Innovación","grantId":"1515","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Coffea","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EN","qualifierName":"enzymology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Indoleacetic Acids","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Ribonucleoproteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Plant Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Base Sequence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Indole-3-acetic acid","Yucca","Cas9","Guide Rna","Crispr–rnp"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Indoleacetic Acids","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Plant Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Ribonucleoproteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"indoleacetic acid","registryNumber":"6U1S09C61L"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00425-026-05115-7"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-06","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-06","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-06","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-06","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-07","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42855940","source":"MED","pmid":"42855940","doi":"10.1177/10445498261495808","title":"Efficient Generation of Single- and Multigene CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout in Human Cells.","authorString":"Manzoor Y, Bejaoui Y, Ramotar D, Aouida M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Manzoor Y","lastName":"Manzoor","firstName":"Yusra","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Pluripotent Stem Cell Disease Modeling, Translational Medicine Department, Research Branch, Sidra Medicine, Doha, Qatar."}]}},{"fullName":"Bejaoui Y","lastName":"Bejaoui","firstName":"Yosra","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Weill Cornell Medicine, Doha, Qatar."}]}},{"fullName":"Ramotar D","lastName":"Ramotar","firstName":"Dindial","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biological and Biomedical Sciences Division, College of Health & Life Sciences, Hamad Bin Khalifa University, Doha, Qatar."}]}},{"fullName":"Aouida M","lastName":"Aouida","firstName":"Mustapha","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7945-5320"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Biological and Biomedical Sciences Division, College of Health & Life Sciences, Hamad Bin Khalifa University, Doha, Qatar."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7945-5320"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4290970,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"DNA Cell Biol","title":"DNA and cell biology","essn":"1557-7430","issn":"1044-5498","nlmid":"9004522","isoabbreviation":"DNA Cell Biol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"10445498261495808","abstractText":"The CRISPR-Cas9 system has revolutionized genome editing, but generating multiple gene knockouts (multi-KOs) remains challenging. This study presents an improved method for efficient generation of multi-KOs using sgRNA DNA strands and the CRISPR/Cas9 system. The method was tested on genes involved in the apoptosis pathway of HEK293T cells. The crRNA for each target gene was designed from Exon 1, near the starting codon ATG, such that a small deletion or insertion in this region would lead to a premature STOP codon and functional knockout. The T7 endonuclease I assay confirmed the high editing efficiency of sgRNAs in both single- and multiple-gene knockouts. Deep sequencing analysis revealed frameshift mutations and premature stop codons, indicating successful loss-of-function mutations. Using the sgRNA DNA strands provided a more uniform and efficient editing profile compared to other methods. This improved protocol offers a simpler, faster, and cost-effective approach for generating multigene knockout clones, with implications for functional genomics, therapeutic development, and synthetic biology.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Pluripotent Stem Cell Disease Modeling, Translational Medicine Department, Research Branch, Sidra Medicine, Doha, Qatar.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Human Cells","Crispr-cas9","Single Knockout","Multiple Knockout"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1177/10445498261495808"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-10","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-10","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-17","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42634520","source":"MED","pmid":"42634520","doi":"10.2174/0113816128481888260727052548","title":"CRISPR-CAS9-Mediated Genetic Enhancement and Metabolic Engineering of Medicinal Crops: An Integrative Review.","authorString":"Soni L, Singhai H, Rathee S, Soni S, Patil UK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Soni L","lastName":"Soni","firstName":"Lokendra","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-2153-3487"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya (A Central University), Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, 470003, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Singhai H","lastName":"Singhai","firstName":"Harshita","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-2602-9322"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya (A Central University), Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, 470003, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Rathee S","lastName":"Rathee","firstName":"Sunny","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2896-5982"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya (A Central University), Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, 470003, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Soni S","lastName":"Soni","firstName":"Sakshi","initials":"S","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9143-0334"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya (A Central University), Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, 470003, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Patil UK","lastName":"Patil","firstName":"Umesh K","initials":"UK","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2096-9922"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya (A Central University), Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, 470003, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2096-9922"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-9143-0334"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-2896-5982"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-2602-9322"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-2153-3487"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4247730,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Pharm Des","title":"Current pharmaceutical design","essn":"1873-4286","issn":"1381-6128","nlmid":"9602487","isoabbreviation":"Curr Pharm Des"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"This descriptive review comprehensively examines recent advances in the genetic improvement of medicinal plants through the application of CRISPR-Cas9 genome-editing technology, highlighting its transformative potential in biotechnology and pharmacognosy. The review systematically discusses the fundamental principles of the CRISPR-Cas9 system, including its mechanism of action, delivery strategies, and comparative advantages over conventional genome-editing techniques. Particular emphasis is placed on the genetic enhancement of medicinal plant species to improve the qualitative and quantitative production of bioactive secondary metabolites through targeted modification of key genetic traits. In addition, the review explores the application of CRISPR-Cas9 for enhancing stress tolerance, environmental adaptability, and the regulation of metabolic biosynthetic pathways. Representative case studies involving medicinal plant species, including Artemisia annua and Catharanthus roseus, are critically evaluated to demonstrate the successful implementation of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated genome editing. Furthermore, the review discusses the synergistic integration of metabolic engineering, omics technologies, and synthetic biology for optimizing secondary metabolite biosynthesis. Finally, it highlights the growing role of next-generation genome-editing technologies, particularly CRISPR-Cas9, in advancing sustainable phytopharmaceutical and nutraceutical research and facilitating the development of high-value medicinal plant resources.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Dr. Harisingh Gour Vishwavidyalaya (A Central University), Sagar, Madhya Pradesh, 470003, India.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Secondary metabolites","Functional genomics","Phytopharmaceuticals","Crispr-cas9 Technology","Biosynthetic Pathway Optimization"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2174/0113816128481888260727052548"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-24","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-03","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-03","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42634056","source":"MED","pmid":"42634056","doi":"10.1007/s13353-026-01108-0","title":"SLC25A38 gene modification mediated by CRISPR/Cas9 in HEK293T cell line.","authorString":"Yazdanparast S, Galehdari H, Khatami S, Hoveizi E.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Yazdanparast S","lastName":"Yazdanparast","firstName":"Shokoufeh","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Faculty of Sciences, Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz, Ahvaz, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Galehdari H","lastName":"Galehdari","firstName":"Hamid","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Faculty of Sciences, Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz, Ahvaz, Iran."}]}},{"fullName":"Khatami S","lastName":"Khatami","firstName":"SaeidReza","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Faculty of Sciences, Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz, Ahvaz, Iran. srkhatami@scu.ac.ir."}]}},{"fullName":"Hoveizi E","lastName":"Hoveizi","firstName":"Elham","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biology, Faculty of Sciences, Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz, Ahvaz, Iran."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4238722,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Appl Genet","title":"Journal of applied genetics","essn":"2190-3883","issn":"1234-1983","nlmid":"9514582","isoabbreviation":"J Appl Genet"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Sideroblastic anemia is a rare disease and one of the heterogeneous bone marrow disorders. Gene therapy is considered as a method for treating these patients. The CRISPR/Cas9 system can recognize specific genomic DNA sequences and create Double-Strand Breaks (DSBs) in DNA. In this study, we attempted to investigate the effect of different sgRNAs in creating targeted cleavage in the desired region using the CRISPR/Cas9 system and inducing changes in the disease-related gene. Following the design of six sgRNAs, recombinant pX458 and pX459 plasmids harboring the respective sgRNA sequences were constructed and transfected into HEK293T cells. After transfection, the efficiency of targeted cleavage of the designed sgRNAs was evaluated using polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE)-based analysis of the target amplicons and subsequently confirmed by Sanger sequencing. Based on the sequencing results, it was shown that in three out of four samples examined, the CRISPR/Cas9 system successfully created targeted disruption at the desired site. Here, we report that the choice of sgRNA target site affects its targeted cleavage activity and show that the combination of guideRNA with SpCas9 can effectively delete the exonic fragment of the SLC25A38 gene in HEK293T cells.","affiliation":"Department of Biology, Faculty of Sciences, Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz, Ahvaz, Iran.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Shahid Chamran University of Ahvaz","grantId":"SCU.SB 1402.90","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Crispr/cas9","Hek293t Cell","Single-guide Rna (Sgrna)","Congenital Sideroblastic Anemia (Csa)","Slc25a38 Gene","Px459"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s13353-026-01108-0"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-23","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-23","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-24","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42973349","source":"MED","pmid":"42973349","doi":"10.2174/0113894501509343261013070100","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-based Genome Editing in Inherited Diseases: Mechanisms, Therapeutic Advances, Safety and Clinical Translation.","authorString":"Fatima J, Siddique YH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Fatima J","lastName":"Fatima","firstName":"Javeria","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Alternative Animal Models, Section of Genetics, Department of Zoology, Faculty of Life Sciences, Aligarh Muslim University, Aligarh, 202002, Uttar Pradesh, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Siddique YH","lastName":"Siddique","firstName":"Yasir Hasan","initials":"YH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7083-3900"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Laboratory of Alternative Animal Models, Section of Genetics, Department of Zoology, Faculty of Life Sciences, Aligarh Muslim University, Aligarh, 202002, Uttar Pradesh, India."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-7083-3900"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4291232,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Curr Drug Targets","title":"Current drug targets","essn":"1873-5592","issn":"1389-4501","nlmid":"100960531","isoabbreviation":"Curr Drug Targets"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Gene therapy has transformed modern biomedical research by enabling curative strategies that target the molecular basis of disease. Among genome-editing technologies, the CRISPR/Cas9 system has emerged as a versatile and efficient platform due to its programmability, simplicity, and precision. This review summarizes the evolution of gene therapy and highlights the therapeutic potential of CRISPR-based genome editing. CRISPR/Cas9 has demonstrated significant potential in correcting disease-causing mutations across multiple inherited disorders, including sickle cell disease, β- thalassemia, familial hypercholesterolemia, phenylketonuria, muscular dystrophies, and cystic fibrosis. Emerging technologies such as base editing and prime editing further improved editing precision and reduced unintended genomic alterations. However, challenges including off-target effects, DNA repair pathway bias, immune responses, and vector-associated toxicities remain major limitations. The review highlights the rapid progress of CRISPR-mediated therapeutics and emphasizes the importance of improving editing accuracy, delivery efficiency, and long-term safety. Advances in vector engineering and genome-editing platforms are expected to overcome current technical and ethical barriers, facilitating broader clinical applications. CRISPR/Cas9 and related genome- editing technologies represent promising disease-modifying and potentially curative therapeutic approaches. Continued optimization of precision, safety, and delivery systems will be essential for successful translation into routine clinical practice.","affiliation":"Laboratory of Alternative Animal Models, Section of Genetics, Department of Zoology, Faculty of Life Sciences, Aligarh Muslim University, Aligarh, 202002, Uttar Pradesh, India.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Non-homologous end joining","Inherited Diseases","Crispr/cas9","Base Editing","Prime Editing"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.2174/0113894501509343261013070100"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"N","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-11-03","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-03","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42906550","source":"MED","pmid":"42906550","doi":"10.1016/j.chom.2026.09.017","title":"A phage-encoded small RNA that mimics chimeric guide to inhibit CRISPR-Cas9.","authorString":"Chen Y, Zhao P, Guo C, Chen D, Yun C, Chen J, Liu L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chen Y","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Ying","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Cellular Stress Biology, Department of Otorhinolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Xiang'an Hospital of Xiamen University, School of Life Sciences, Faculty of Medicine and Life Sciences, Xiamen University, Xiamen, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao P","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Peng","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Natural and Biomimetic Drugs & Department of Biophysics, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Peking University, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Guo C","lastName":"Guo","firstName":"Chenmin","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Cellular Stress Biology, Department of Otorhinolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Xiang'an Hospital of Xiamen University, School of Life Sciences, Faculty of Medicine and Life Sciences, Xiamen University, Xiamen, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen D","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Dandan","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Natural and Biomimetic Drugs & Department of Biophysics, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Peking University, Beijing, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Yun C","lastName":"Yun","firstName":"Caihong","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5880-8307"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Natural and Biomimetic Drugs & Department of Biophysics, School of Basic Medical Sciences, Peking University, Beijing, China. Electronic address: yunch@pku.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen J","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Jiyun","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Cellular Stress Biology, Department of Otorhinolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Xiang'an Hospital of Xiamen University, School of Life Sciences, Faculty of Medicine and Life Sciences, Xiamen University, Xiamen, China. Electronic address: chenjiyun@xmu.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Liu L","lastName":"Liu","firstName":"Liang","initials":"L","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5379-0638"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Cellular Stress Biology, Department of Otorhinolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Xiang'an Hospital of Xiamen University, School of Life Sciences, Faculty of Medicine and Life Sciences, Xiamen University, Xiamen, China. Electronic address: liangliu2019@xmu.edu.cn."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5379-0638"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-5880-8307"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4285301,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Cell Host Microbe","title":"Cell host & microbe","essn":"1934-6069","issn":"1931-3128","nlmid":"101302316","isoabbreviation":"Cell Host Microbe"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"S1931-3128(26)00406-3","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9 relies on a dual CRISPR RNA (crRNA)-trans-activating crRNA (tracrRNA) guide, yet whether RNA-based anti-CRISPRs exist for this system has remained unknown. Here, we identify a phage-encoded small non-coding RNA, rAcrIIA1, that adopts an unexpected chimeric crRNA-tracrRNA architecture, faithfully recapitulating the entire single-guide RNA (sgRNA) of Cas9. Cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM) structures of the Cas9-rAcrIIA1 binary complex and the Cas9-rAcrIIA1-DNA ternary complex reveal that rAcrIIA1 engages Cas9 nearly identically to the native sgRNA, competitively blocking crRNA and tracrRNA loading and thereby abolishing DNA interference. Strikingly, beyond its natural inhibitory function, rAcrIIA1 is fully reprogrammable: with variable guide sequences, it directs Cas9 for sequence-specific DNA cleavage with efficiency comparable to the canonical sgRNA, and it activates trans-cleavage more potently. Thus, rAcrIIA1 provides an experimentally validated, mechanistically resolved RNA-based inhibitor of CRISPR-Cas9, reveals a distinct anti-CRISPR mechanism through complete sgRNA mimicry, and serves as a versatile bifunctional scaffold for genome editing and diagnostics.","affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Cellular Stress Biology, Department of Otorhinolaryngology Head and Neck Surgery, Xiang'an Hospital of Xiamen University, School of Life Sciences, Faculty of Medicine and Life Sciences, Xiamen University, Xiamen, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Structure","Phage","Small Non-coding Rna","Crispr-cas9 System","Single-guide Rna","Rna-based Anti-crispr"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chom.2026.09.017"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"PPR","id":"PPR1245105","type":"Preprint in","note":"Link created based on a title-first author match","orderIn":10002}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-22","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-23","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42469418","source":"MED","pmid":"42469418","doi":"10.1038/s41390-026-05270-3","title":"The future of pediatric gene therapy: CRISPR-Cas9, AI, and personalized medicine.","authorString":"Pan Y, Ding J, Wang W, Xu D, Fan J, Zhang W.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Pan Y","lastName":"Pan","firstName":"Yinping","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ding J","lastName":"Ding","firstName":"Jian","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Electrodiagnosis, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang W","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Wenhai","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Xu D","lastName":"Xu","firstName":"Dexin","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Orthopedics, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Fan J","lastName":"Fan","firstName":"Jihuan","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Teaching and Research, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang W","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Wenbin","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3024-5415"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cardiology, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China. zwb80311a@sina.com."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-3024-5415"}]},"journalInfo":{"journalIssueId":4223269,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Pediatr Res","title":"Pediatric research","essn":"1530-0447","issn":"0031-3998","nlmid":"0100714","isoabbreviation":"Pediatr Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"The future of pediatric gene therapy is being fundamentally reshaped by the convergence of CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing, artificial intelligence (AI), and personalized medicine, providing unprecedented opportunities for the treatment of rare and previously intractable genetic disorders in children. CRISPR-Cas9 enables precise and potentially curative correction of pathogenic mutations underlying conditions, such as sickle cell disease, cystic fibrosis, and Duchenne muscular dystrophy, with particularly notable progress in hematological disorders. AI-driven tools, like DeepCRISPR and CRISPR-GPT are emerging as valuable assets for streamlining experimental design, prioritizing candidate edits, and accelerating preclinical-to-clinical translation. In parallel, advances in delivery systems, including adeno-associated viral (AAV) vectors and non-viral platforms such as lipid nanoparticles (LNPs), are expanding the feasibility of both ex vivo and in vivo pediatric applications. The synergistic integration of CRISPR-Cas9, AI, and personalized medicine enables more precise, patient-specific therapeutic strategies that better account for the developmental, physiological, and genetic heterogeneity of pediatric populations. Addressing these challenges will require interdisciplinary collaboration across genome engineering, AI science, clinical pediatrics, bioethics, and regulatory policy. The establishment of standardized regulatory frameworks, accompanied by robust and transparent AI integration, will be essential to ensure safe, equitable, and scalable translation. IMPACT: The future of pediatric gene therapy is being reshaped by the integration of CRISPR-Cas9, AI, and personalized medicine. We review recent clinical trials, including ex vivo CRISPR-Cas9 applications for hemoglobinopathies. This article emphasizes interdisciplinary collaboration to overcome these barriers. Pediatric gene therapy holds transformative potential to provide curative solutions for children.","affiliation":"Department of Pediatrics, Jilin Province FAW General Hospital, Changchun, China.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41390-026-05270-3"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-07-17","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-07-18","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42759108","source":"MED","pmid":"42759108","doi":"10.1016/j.plaphy.2026.111740","title":"CRISPR/Cas9 engineering: Insights into tomato fruit development, stress resilience via brassinosteroid pathways.","authorString":"Tahira M, Su W, Maqsood FK, Ikram M, Javaid MH, Rao MJ, Zhang J.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Tahira M","lastName":"Tahira","firstName":"Maryam","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Key Laboratory for Germplasm Innovation & Utilization of Horticultural Crops, College of Horticulture and Forestry, Huazhong Agricultural University, Wuhan, 430070, China. Electronic address: maryam@webmail.hzau.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Su W","lastName":"Su","firstName":"Wenran","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Key Laboratory for Germplasm Innovation & Utilization of Horticultural Crops, College of Horticulture and Forestry, Huazhong Agricultural University, Wuhan, 430070, China. Electronic address: Suwenran@163.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Maqsood FK","lastName":"Maqsood","firstName":"Faizan Khurram","initials":"FK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"College of Plant Sciences, Department of Plant Protection, Huazhong Agriculture University, Wuhan, 430070, China. Electronic address: faizan.khuram.pk@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Ikram M","lastName":"Ikram","firstName":"Muhammad","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Engineering Research Center for Sugarcane, Key Laboratory of Sugarcane Biology and Genetic Breeding, Ministry of Agriculture and Rural Affairs, Fujian Agriculture and Forestry University, Fuzhou, 350002, China. Electronic address: r4rana88@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Javaid MH","lastName":"Javaid","firstName":"Muhammad Haseeb","initials":"MH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Vegetable Sciences, College of Agriculture and Biotechnology, Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310058, China. Electronic address: haseeb.javaid@zju.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Rao MJ","lastName":"Rao","firstName":"Muhammad Junaid","initials":"MJ","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Key Laboratory for Development and Utilization of Forest Food Resources, Zhejiang A&F University, Hangzhou, Zhejiang, 311300, China. Electronic address: mjr@zafu.edu.cn."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang J","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Junhong","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"National Key Laboratory for Germplasm Innovation & Utilization of Horticultural Crops, College of Horticulture and Forestry, Huazhong Agricultural University, Wuhan, 430070, China. Electronic address: zhangjunhng@mail.hzau.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"238","journalIssueId":4257496,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Plant Physiol Biochem","title":"Plant physiology and biochemistry : PPB","essn":"1873-2690","issn":"0981-9428","nlmid":"9882449","isoabbreviation":"Plant Physiol Biochem"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"111740","abstractText":"Tomato (Solanum lycopersicum), a cornerstone of global agriculture and nutrition, has undergone decades of breeding focused on yield and stress resilience. Yet, consumer-driven traits such as fruit uniformity, flavor complexity, and nutritional value remain suboptimal. The emerging convergence of hormonal biology and precision genome editing presents a transformative approach to addressing this gap. Brassinosteroids (BRs), a class of steroidal phytohormones, act as central regulators of cell expansion, tissue patterning, and developmental plasticity. Their signaling cascade, initiated by perception at the BRI1-BAK1 receptor complex and transduced via BES1/BZR1 transcriptional modules, intersects with networks controlling fruit set, morphology, ripening, and stress adaptation. Gene editing through CRISPR/Cas9 technology now enables targeted dissection and manipulation of these BR-regulated nodes with unprecedented accuracy. CRISPR/Cas9 studies have directly characterized BR signaling regulators such as SlBZR1, SlBIN2, and SlBES1, while complementary genetic and transgenic studies have provided functional evidence for other BR-related components, including SlBRI1 and the BR-biosynthetic gene SlDWF4, further supporting the roles of BR signaling in tomato fruit development, ripening, and carotenoid accumulation. Beyond improvement of traits, CRISPR/Cas9 offers the potential to tune BR pathways and their crosstalk with auxin and ethylene, providing a systems-level framework for engineering climate-resilient and nutritionally superior cultivars. This review integrates mechanistic insights into BR signaling with cutting-edge CRISPR/Cas9 applications, positioning tomato as a model for reprogramming fruit development and as a paradigm for next-generation crop improvement.","affiliation":"National Key Laboratory for Germplasm Innovation & Utilization of Horticultural Crops, College of Horticulture and Forestry, Huazhong Agricultural University, Wuhan, 430070, China. Electronic address: maryam@webmail.hzau.edu.cn.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plants, Genetically Modified"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Fruit","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GD","qualifierName":"growth & development"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plant Growth Regulators","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plant Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Signal Transduction"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Stress, Physiological"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Brassinosteroids","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Gene Editing"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Solanum lycopersicum","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GD","qualifierName":"growth & development"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Brassinosteroid Signaling","Genome Editing","Crispr-cas9","Fruit Quality Enhancement","Tomato Morphogenesis"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Plant Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Plant Growth Regulators","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Brassinosteroids","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.plaphy.2026.111740"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-30","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-30","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-09","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-20","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1339230","source":"PPR","doi":"10.20944/preprints202610.1461.v1","title":"Therapeutic Horizons of CRISPR/Cas9 Genome Editing in Overcoming Bacterial Antimicrobial Resistance: A Comprehensive Narrative Review","authorString":"Mehari MG.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Mehari MG","lastName":"Mehari","firstName":"Molla Getie","initials":"MG"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Bacterial antimicrobial resistance (AMR) represents a global public health crisis that threatens to render conventional antibiotic regimens obsolete. The clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats-associated protein 9 (CRISPR/Cas9) system has emerged as a revolutionary tool in molecular biology, offering unmatched sequence-specific genome-editing capabilities. By leveraging custom single-guide RNAs (sgRNAs), CRISPR/Cas9 can precisely target, cleave, or modify specific genomic and plasmid-borne resistance determinants, thereby neutralizing resistance pathways or directly inducing lethal double-strand breaks in pathogenic bacteria. Beyond simple gene disruption, CRISPR-based tools can eliminate resistance-carrying plasmids, block horizontal gene transfer (HGT), impair virulence pathways, and dismantle biofilm architectures while preserving commensal microflora. This narrative review provides a rigorous, updated synthesis of the structural and mechanical framework of CRISPR/Cas9, its targeted application across major multidrug-resistant (MDR) pathogens, a side-by-side analysis of various delivery systems, and the current balance of therapeutic promise against biological, ecological, and translational safety risks. Furthermore, we outline synergistic integrations with advanced nanocarriers, bacteriophage platforms, synthetic biology, and antimicrobial peptides (AMPs), laying out a prospective roadmap toward publishable clinical translation.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Preprints.org","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.20944/preprints202610.1461.v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-15","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-15","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-16","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1339230","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-10-15","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42886223","source":"MED","pmid":"42886223","doi":"10.1021/acsami.6c11615","title":"High-Throughput Screening of a Lipid Nanoparticle (LNP) Library Identifies Lead Formulations for CRISPR-Cas9 Ribonucleoprotein Delivery and Gene Editing.","authorString":"Wang Y, Haghighat Naeini A, Kumar N, Zhao M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wang Y","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Yu","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, University of Kansas, Lawrence, Kansas66047, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Haghighat Naeini A","lastName":"Haghighat Naeini","firstName":"Amin","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-9035-5050"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, University of Kansas, Lawrence, Kansas66047, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Kumar N","lastName":"Kumar","firstName":"Nishant","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, University of Kansas, Lawrence, Kansas66047, United States."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhao M","lastName":"Zhao","firstName":"Ming","initials":"M","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-2704-8036"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, University of Kansas, Lawrence, Kansas66047, United States."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0002-9035-5050"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0004-2704-8036"}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"42","volume":"18","journalIssueId":4300245,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"ACS Appl Mater Interfaces","title":"ACS applied materials & interfaces","essn":"1944-8252","issn":"1944-8244","nlmid":"101504991","isoabbreviation":"ACS Appl Mater Interfaces"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"57043-57056","abstractText":"Delivery of CRISPR-Cas9 ribonucleoprotein (RNP) using synthetic materials can bypass the host translational machinery and limit the duration of genomic exposure, minimizing the risk of off-target mutations. Despite their advantages, lipid nanoparticle (LNP) platforms for CRISPR-Cas9 RNP delivery that achieve highly efficient editing at low doses remain limited. In the present study, we developed a library of ionizable lipids (ILs) and corresponding LNPs to enhance the delivery and editing efficiencies of CRISPR-Cas9 RNP complexes. Following comprehensive in vitro screening and formulation optimization, an optimal LNP candidate containing the C12A9 IL (denoted as LNPC12A9) was identified that achieved ∼90% EGFP disruption in HEK293T-EGFP cells at an RNP concentration of 30 nM and ∼60% at 15 nM. The editing performance was consistently observed at an RNP concentration of 15 nM when editing the Gcgr and HPRT1 genes across multiple hepatic cell lines. Moreover, coencapsulation of RNP and a single-stranded oligodeoxynucleotide (ssODN) template for homology-directed repair (HDR) within LNPC12A9 induced an HDR rate of 37.8%. Systemic administration of LNPC12A9 into C57BL/6J mice led to liver tropism and a total hepatic Pcsk9 gene disruption rate of 1.39%. In conclusion, this study provides insights into the construction of an LNP platform to improve the delivery efficiency of CRISPR-Cas9 RNP, thereby facilitating the development of LNPs for safe, precise, and tissue-specific Cas9 RNP-based genome editing therapies.","affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, University of Kansas, Lawrence, Kansas66047, United States.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Mice"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Lipids","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Ribonucleoproteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","a