 neutrophils, we investigated how CRISPR/Cas9-mediated kindlin-3 deletion affects neutrophil characteristics. CRISPR/Cas9 targeting FERMT3 resulted in an ∼80-bp deletion within exon 2, impairing protein production. FERMT3 KO neutrophils retained normal in vitro development and phenotype with similar surface marker expression, nuclear morphology, and expansion potential compared with untreated cultured neutrophils. Loss of kindlin-3 abolished zymosan-induced NADPH oxidase activity, recapitulating the ROS production defect of LAD-III neutrophils. However, although basal adhesion was reduced, stimulus-induced integrin activity remained largely intact. FERMT3 KO neutrophils retained fMLP-driven chemotaxis while showing impaired migration in response to C5a. Together, these findings show that disruption of kindlin-3 largely recapitulates the LAD-III phenotype while exerting some effector functions upon stimulation without intact kindlin-3.","affiliation":"Department of Research, Sanquin Blood Supply Foundation, Amsterdam, Netherlands.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"E-Rare-3","grantId":"355527","orderIn":0},{"agency":"E-Rare-3","grantId":"JTC2018SF","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Dutch Ministry of Health","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Sanquin Blood Supply Product and Process Development Cellular Products Fund","orderIn":0},{"agency":"E-Rare-3","grantId":"326358","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.70962/jhi.20260086"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13685712"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13685712?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["refseq"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-22","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-22","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-24","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-23","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-24","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42601370","source":"MED","pmid":"42601370","pmcid":"PMC13476449","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13476449"]},"doi":"10.1038/s41598-026-61844-5","title":"Design and transfection of CRISPR/Cas9 constructs for the myostatin gene in Labeo rohita muscle cells.","authorString":"Iqbal G, Pinto N, Pawaskar D, Sonwane AA, Rasal KD, Singh LS, Chikkathimmashetty NN, Goswami M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Iqbal G","lastName":"Iqbal","firstName":"Gowhar","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Pinto N","lastName":"Pinto","firstName":"Nevil","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Pawaskar D","lastName":"Pawaskar","firstName":"Darshan","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Sonwane AA","lastName":"Sonwane","firstName":"Arvind A","initials":"AA","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Rasal KD","lastName":"Rasal","firstName":"Kiran D","initials":"KD","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Singh LS","lastName":"Singh","firstName":"Lukram Sushil","initials":"LS","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Chikkathimmashetty NN","lastName":"Chikkathimmashetty","firstName":"Nidarshan N","initials":"NN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Goswami M","lastName":"Goswami","firstName":"Mukunda","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India. mukugoswami@gmail.com."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"16","journalIssueId":4223254,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Jul","monthOfPublication":7,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Sci Rep","title":"Scientific reports","essn":"2045-2322","issn":"2045-2322","nlmid":"101563288","isoabbreviation":"Sci Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-07-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"25509","abstractText":"Myostatin (mstn) is a negative regulator of skeletal muscle growth and is considered as an important target for enhancing aquaculture production. The present study aimed to design and validate single-guide RNAs (sgRNAs) and CRISPR/Cas9 constructs for exon 1 of the mstnb gene in Labeo rohita, and to evaluate their transfection efficiency in the L. rohita dorsal muscle (LRDM) cell line at the 10th, 20th, and 30th passages. sgRNAs were designed and cloned into the pSpCas9(BB)-2A-GFP (PX458) vector using BbsI restriction digestion and ligation. Successful insertion and correct orientation of the sgRNAs were confirmed through Sanger sequencing. LRDM cells were revived and maintained in L-15 medium supplemented with 10% fetal bovine serum. Transfection was performed at the 10th, 20th, and 30th passages. Distinct GFP-positive cells were observed at all passages for both sgRNA constructs, indicating the ability of the developed cell line to successfully express the constructs across different passages. The study successfully established CRISPR/Cas9 plasmid constructs for the mstnb gene in L. rohita and demonstrated their transfection in LRDM cell line across multiple passages. These findings provide a basis for future studies on genome editing approaches using CRISPR/Cas9 constructs in fish muscle cell lines and highlight the potential application of CRISPR/Cas9 technology for genetic engineering applications in fish muscle cells.","affiliation":"Division of Fish Genetics and Biotechnology, ICAR - Central Institute of Fisheries Education, Panch Marg, Yari Road, Mumbai, 400061, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cell Line"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Muscle Cells","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Cyprinidae","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Transfection","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Myostatin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Transfection","Muscle cells","Labeo Rohita","Crispr/cas9","Sgrnas","Mstnb","Pspcas9(bb)-2a-gfp Plasmid"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Myostatin","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"RNA, Guide, CRISPR-Cas Systems","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-61844-5"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13476449"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13476449?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["refseq","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-14","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-14","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-17","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-25","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-25","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-16","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-09","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42303162","source":"MED","pmid":"42303162","doi":"10.1016/j.jbiotec.2026.06.012","title":"CRISPR Cas9 revolutionizing genetic engineering and therapeutic applications.","authorString":"Durairaj S, Durairaj S, Krishnan S, Raju A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Durairaj S","lastName":"Durairaj","firstName":"Sivakumar","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Agricultural Engineering, Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education, Krishnankoil 626126, Tamil Nadu, India. Electronic address: d.sivakumar@klu.ac.in."}]}},{"fullName":"Durairaj S","lastName":"Durairaj","firstName":"Shankar","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Pharmaceutical Chemistry, K.M. College of Pharmacy, Madurai 625107, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Krishnan S","lastName":"Krishnan","firstName":"Sundar","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biotechnology, Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education, Krishnankoil 626126, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Raju A","lastName":"Raju","firstName":"Anand","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Civil and Architectural Engineering Unit, University of Technology and Applied Sciences, Al Khuwair 133, Muscat, Oman."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5228-0145"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-7156-1057"}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"417","journalIssueId":4228342,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Biotechnol","title":"Journal of biotechnology","essn":"1873-4863","issn":"0168-1656","nlmid":"8411927","isoabbreviation":"J Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"246-264","abstractText":"Genetic engineering has been transformed by CRISPR-Cas9 technology, offering high precision and adaptability in biological research and therapeutic innovation. Originating from a bacterial defense system, CRISPR-Cas9 enables targeted DNA editing through guide RNA-directed Cas9 nuclease activity, allowing gene modification, mutation correction, and disease mechanism analysis. This has opened new avenues in personalized medicine and gene therapy, particularly for cancer and inherited disorders, alongside applications in agriculture. In oncology, CRISPR-Cas9 demonstrates strong potential in oncogene targeting, immune cell engineering, and CAR-T-based immunotherapy, supported by substantial preclinical success. Delivery efficiency is enhanced through systems such as exosomes, liposomes, and nanoparticles, improving stability and tumor targeting. However, clinical translation remains constrained by off-target effects, delivery limitations, and ethical concerns in human genome editing, particularly germline modification. CRISPR shows therapeutic promise for muscular dystrophy, sickle cell disease, and cystic fibrosis. Emerging platforms including base editing, prime editing, and dCas9-based epigenome editing enable precise genome and gene regulation without double-strand breaks, reducing toxicity and expanding therapeutic scope in cancer and genetic diseases. Regulatory frameworks remain heterogeneous, affecting translation. The United States leads in approvals and clinical progress, the European Union emphasizes safety and ethics, China shows rapid expansion in clinical trials, and India remains in early stages due to regulatory and infrastructure constraints. Public perception influences adoption, shaped by misinformation and limited awareness. Persistent gaps in long-term safety, clinical efficacy, and population diversity remain challenges. Overall, CRISPR-Cas9 represents a transformative but carefully regulated platform for advancing biotechnology and medicine.","affiliation":"Department of Agricultural Engineering, Kalasalingam Academy of Research and Education, Krishnankoil 626126, Tamil Nadu, India. Electronic address: d.sivakumar@klu.ac.in.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"University of Technology and Applied Sciences - Muscat","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Genetic Engineering","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Genetic Therapy","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Therapeutic Applications","Ethical Considerations","Genome Editing","Guide Rna","Targeted Editing","Crispr-cas9 Nuclease"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbiotec.2026.06.012"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-07-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-16","dateOfRevision":"2026-07-08","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-06-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-18","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1348821","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-11250691/v1","title":"MOET-assisted CRISPR/Cas9-mediated MSTN genome editing in caprine embryos by electroporation and embryo transfer","authorString":"Singh SP, Pathak J, Pathak M, Goel A, Soni YK, Chatli MK.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Singh SP","lastName":"Singh","firstName":"Shiva Pratap","initials":"SP"},{"fullName":"Pathak J","lastName":"Pathak","firstName":"Juhi","initials":"J"},{"fullName":"Pathak M","lastName":"Pathak","firstName":"Manisha","initials":"M"},{"fullName":"Goel A","lastName":"Goel","firstName":"Anjana","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Soni YK","lastName":"Soni","firstName":"Yogesh Kumar","initials":"YK"},{"fullName":"Chatli MK","lastName":"Chatli","firstName":"Manish Kumar","initials":"MK"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>  The present study aimed to establish a CRISPR/Cas9-mediated approach for targeted modification of the myostatin (MSTN) gene in MOET-derived caprine presumptive zygotes using electroporation. An sgRNA targeting exon 1 of MSTN was complexed with recombinant Cas9 protein to form a ribonucleoprotein (RNP) complex. A total of 78 presumptive zygotes recovered laparoscopically from eight superovulated donor does were electroporated using three square-wave pulses at 25 V, 3 ms duration, and 100 ms intervals. Following  <italic>in vitro</italic>  culture, all embryos developed to the morula stage, with two reaching the blastocyst stage. T7E1 analysis of four randomly selected morulae revealed cleavage at the targeted locus in three embryos (75%). Four 4-cell embryos were transferred into a synchronized Sirohi recipient, resulting in pregnancy and recovery of two morphologically normal fetuses on Day 57. T7E1 analysis detected cleavage at the targeted locus in one fetus. These findings demonstrate the potential of integrating MOET with CRISPR/Cas9 RNP electroporation for targeted genome editing in caprine embryos.  </p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-11250691/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-24","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-23","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-24","versionList":{"version":[{"id":"PPR1348821","source":"PPR","versionNumber":1,"firstPublishDate":"2026-10-23","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["preprint"]},"hasEvaluations":"N"}]},"versionNumber":1,"hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42895217","source":"MED","pmid":"42895217","doi":"10.1111/jipb.70422","title":"Creation of fragrant wheat by targeted mutagenesis of either BADH1 or both BADH1 and BADH2 using CRISPR/Cas9.","authorString":"Luo J, Yan L, Li J, Zhang C, Li S, He Y, Guo Y, Xia L.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Luo J","lastName":"Luo","firstName":"Jinman","initials":"J","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0006-3137-8526"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Crop Gene Resources and Breeding, Institute of Crop Sciences (ICS), Chinese Academy of Agricultural Sciences (CAAS), Beijing, 100081, China."},{"affiliation":"State Key Laboratory of Plant Environmental Resilience, College of Biological Sciences, China Agricultural University, Beijing, 100