ord":["Immunotherapy","BCG","Bladder cancer","MMP9","Tumor Microenvironment","Pd‐l1","Crispr‐cas9"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"BCG Vaccine","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Matrix Metalloproteinase 9","registryNumber":"EC 3.4.24.35"},{"name":"Cd274 protein, mouse","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Mmp9 protein, mouse","registryNumber":"EC 3.4.24.35"},{"name":"B7-H1 Antigen","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/iub.70130"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13550818"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13550818?pdf=render"}]},"commentCorrectionList":{"commentCorrection":[{"source":"MED","id":"42852531","reference":"IUBMB Life. 2026 Oct;78(10):e70134. doi: 10.1002/iub.70134.","type":"Erratum in","orderIn":1}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-08","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-08","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-20","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-01","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-13","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1302746","source":"PPR","doi":"10.21203/rs.3.rs-10709958/v1","title":"Beyond HbS and Thalassemia: CRISPR-Cas9 Strategies for Targeting Rare Hemoglobin Gene Mutations","authorString":"Ahsan M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ahsan M","lastName":"Ahsan","firstName":"Muhammad","initials":"M"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"<title>Abstract</title>  <p>Objective  To develop an in-silico CRISPR-Cas9 workflow for identifying candidate guide RNAs (gRNAs) targeting rare HBB mutations beyond HbS and β-thalassemia, with emphasis on variants located in exon 3 and near the 3′ end of HBB. Materials and Methods  Reference and variant HBB sequences were retrieved from the National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI). Candidate CRISPR-Cas9 gRNAs were designed using CHOPCHOP with an NGG PAM, evaluated for predicted off-target activity using Cas-OFFinder against the human GRCh38 genome, and assessed for sequence specificity using BLAST or EMBOSS Needle. Results  The workflow identified candidate gRNAs near the selected mutation region and enabled evaluation of guide efficiency, GC content, self-complementarity, predicted off-target activity, and primer characteristics. The selected guide was reported to have no predicted off-targets in the presented Cas-OFFinder analysis and showed 100% sequence similarity/complementarity across the reported 20-bp target region in the BLAST analysis. Conclusion  A sequential bioinformatics pipeline integrating NCBI, CHOPCHOP, Cas-OFFinder, and sequence alignment provides a practical pre-experimental framework for evaluating CRISPR-Cas9 candidates against rare hemoglobin mutations. The findings are computational and require biological validation before therapeutic application.</p>","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"Research Square","yearOfPublication":2026},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10709958/v1"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":