ina."}]}},{"fullName":"Qiu M","lastName":"Qiu","firstName":"Mengyu","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang K","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Kai","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhai J","lastName":"Zhai","firstName":"Jianjun","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Feng S","lastName":"Feng","firstName":"Shuying","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China."},{"affiliation":"Henan Engineering Research Center for Chinese Medicine Foods for Special Medical Purpose, Zhengzhou, China."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"14","journalIssueId":4103371,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Bioeng Biotechnol","title":"Frontiers in bioengineering and biotechnology","essn":"2296-4185","issn":"2296-4185","nlmid":"101632513","isoabbreviation":"Front Bioeng Biotechnol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1823483","abstractText":"Mesenchymal stem cell-derived exosomes (MSC-Exos) combined with CRISPR-Cas9 hold substantial therapeutic potential for immune-mediated diseases, although significant technical obstacles remain. This review presents a comprehensive analysis of strategies for engineering MSC-Exos as delivery vehicles for CRISPR-Cas9, with emphasis on cargo-loading methodologies and surface modifications that enable targeting specific immune cell populations. We examine the mechanistic basis for the therapeutic effects of these engineered platforms and critically assess the challenges impeding clinical translation, including manufacturing scalability, safety concerns, and regulatory considerations. Key areas of focus for this Research Topic-vehicle engineering, cargo packaging efficiency, biological barriers, and <i>in vivo</i> safety-are systematically addressed. Finally, we discuss emerging directions, including next-generation gene editors and stimulus-responsive biomaterials. This review provides a balanced framework for advancing MSC-Exos-based nanoplatforms toward precision gene therapies for immune disorders.","affiliation":"Medical College, Henan University of Chinese Medicine, Zhengzhou, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Immunotherapy","Autoimmune diseases","Targeted Delivery","Gene Editing","Clinical Translation","Crispr-cas9","Mesenchymal Stem Cell-derived Exosomes","Vehicle Engineering"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2026.1823483"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13527436"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13527436?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42787623","source":"MED","pmid":"42787623","pmcid":"PMC13601907","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13601907"]},"doi":"10.3389/fgeed.2026.1868779","title":"Clinical translation of CRISPR-Cas9 therapeutics in cancer and inherited genetic disorders.","authorString":"Bashir K, Vasudev P, Chhetri V, Dubhashi DP, Teli YG, Biswas R, Ramaiah S, Anbarasu A.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bashir K","lastName":"Bashir","firstName":"Khushi","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Vasudev P","lastName":"Vasudev","firstName":"Prathiksha","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Chhetri V","lastName":"Chhetri","firstName":"Vikas","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Dubhashi DP","lastName":"Dubhashi","firstName":"Devika Prasad","initials":"DP","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Teli YG","lastName":"Teli","firstName":"Yash Gurunath","initials":"YG","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Biswas R","lastName":"Biswas","firstName":"Rhitam","initials":"R","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biotechnology, SBST, VIT, Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Ramaiah S","lastName":"Ramaiah","firstName":"Sudha","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Bio-Sciences, SBST, VIT, Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Anbarasu A","lastName":"Anbarasu","firstName":"Anand","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biotechnology, SBST, VIT, Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4145489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","nlmid":"101775540","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1868779","abstractText":"CRISPR-Cas9, adapted from the bacterial Type II CRISPR adaptive immune system, functions as a programmable RNA-guided endonuclease that employs a single-guide RNA to direct Cas9 to specific genomic loci. CRISPR-Cas9 has transformed targeted genome editing by replacing complex protein engineering with programmable Watson-Crick base pairing between the guide RNA and target DNA. This review was developed following a structured literature search of major biomedical databases and clinical trial registries to synthesize current evidence on the therapeutic applications of CRISPR-Cas9 in oncology and inherited genetic disorders. Clinical studies of <i>ex vivo BCL11A</i>-enhancer editing have shown fetal hemoglobin reactivation, with most evaluable participants with sickle cell disease remaining free of severe vaso-occlusive crises for the prespecified period and most evaluable participants with transfusion-dependent β-thalassemia achieving sustained transfusion independence. <i>In vivo</i> reductions in circulating transthyretin protein levels have been achieved for transthyretin amyloidosis via lipid nanoparticle delivery, while clinically meaningful improvements in selected measures of visual function were observed in a subset of patients receiving subretinal AAV-delivered CRISPR editing for <i>CEP290</i>-associated Leber congenital amaurosis type 10. Preclinical and early clinical studies have further investigated CRISPR-engineered T cells designed to improve antitumor activity, persistence, or resistance to inhibitory signaling. Despite these advances, key translational hurdles include the risk of off-target mutations and large-scale chromosomal rearrangements. Furthermore, immune responses against bacterial Cas9 nucleases and viral delivery vectors may limit the long-term efficacy of CRISPR-based therapies, while technical barriers surrounding delivery to extrahepatic tissues, such as skeletal muscle and the central nervous system, continue to hinder broader clinical success. Ethical concerns regarding germline modifications and the high cost of individualized therapies present additional translational challenges. Consequently, emerging DSB-independent technologies, such as base editing and prime editing, may reduce selected DSB-associated liabilities, but each introduces distinct editing, delivery, and genotoxicity risks that require product-specific evaluation.","affiliation":"Department of Bio-Medical Sciences, School of Bio-Sciences and Technology (SBST), Vellore Institute of Technology (VIT), Vellore, Tamil Nadu, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Gene therapy","Cancer","Genome Editing","Clinical Translation","Crispr-cas9","Inherited Genetic Disorders"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2026.1868779"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601907"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13601907?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-25","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-25","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-26","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-10","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-10","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-26","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-25","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"41670012","source":"MED","pmid":"41670012","doi":"10.1080/17435889.2026.2629031","title":"The application of CRISPR-Cas9 system in brain diseases.","authorString":"Miao Y, Li C, Su Y, Peng T, Wang J, Liu S, Ma C, Li L, Wang Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Miao Y","lastName":"Miao","firstName":"Yaodong","initials":"Y","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-6627-5694"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Endocrinology, Second Affiliated Hospital of Tianjin University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, Tianjin, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Li C","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Chun","initials":"C","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0009-0007-3174-284X"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Tradi