},{"fullName":"Habermann H","lastName":"Habermann","firstName":"Hanna","initials":"H","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Biotechnology and Reproduction of Farm Animals, Burckhardtweg 2, Göttingen, 37077, Germany. Electronic address: hanna.habermann@uni-goettingen.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Haake L","lastName":"Haake","firstName":"Lientje","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Biotechnology and Reproduction of Farm Animals, Burckhardtweg 2, Göttingen, 37077, Germany. Electronic address: lientje-sophie.haake@uni-goettingen.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Brenig B","lastName":"Brenig","firstName":"Bertram","initials":"B","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Molecular Biology of Livestock and Molecular Diagnostics, Burckhardtweg 2, Goettingen, 37077, Germany. Electronic address: bbrenig@gwdg.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Besenfelder U","lastName":"Besenfelder","firstName":"Urban","initials":"U","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Animal Breeding and Genetics, University of Veterinary Medicine Vienna, Vienna, Austria. Electronic address: urban.besenfelder@vetmeduni.ac.at."}]}},{"fullName":"Havlicek V","lastName":"Havlicek","firstName":"Vitezslav","initials":"V","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Institute of Animal Breeding and Genetics, University of Veterinary Medicine Vienna, Vienna, Austria. Electronic address: Vitezslav.Havlicek@vetmeduni.ac.at."}]}},{"fullName":"Tesfaye D","lastName":"Tesfaye","firstName":"Dawit","initials":"D","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Colorado State University Department of Biomedical Sciences, Animal Reproduction and Biotechnology Laboratory, 3105 Rampart Rd, Fort Collins, CO, 80521, USA. Electronic address: Dawit.Tesfaye@colostate.edu."}]}},{"fullName":"Tholen E","lastName":"Tholen","firstName":"Ernst","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Bonn, Institute of Animal Sciences, Endenicher Allee 15, Bonn, 53115, Germany. Electronic address: ernst.tholen@uni-bonn.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Rings F","lastName":"Rings","firstName":"Franca","initials":"F","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Bonn, Institute of Animal Sciences, Endenicher Allee 15, Bonn, 53115, Germany. Electronic address: frings1@uni-bonn.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Held-Hoelker E","lastName":"Held-Hoelker","firstName":"Eva","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Biotechnology and Reproduction of Farm Animals, Burckhardtweg 2, Göttingen, 37077, Germany; University of Bonn, Institute of Animal Sciences, Endenicher Allee 15, Bonn, 53115, Germany. Electronic address: eva.held-hoelker@uni-goettingen.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Ghanem N","lastName":"Ghanem","firstName":"Nasser","initials":"N","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Animal Production, Cairo University, Cairo, Egypt. Electronic address: nassergo@agr.cu.edu.eg."}]}},{"fullName":"Benedetti C","lastName":"Benedetti","firstName":"Camilla","initials":"C","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Biotechnology and Reproduction of Farm Animals, Burckhardtweg 2, Göttingen, 37077, Germany. Electronic address: camilla.benedetti@uni-goettingen.de."}]}},{"fullName":"Hoelker M","lastName":"Hoelker","firstName":"Michael","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Biotechnology and Reproduction of Farm Animals, Burckhardtweg 2, Göttingen, 37077, Germany. Electronic address: michael.hoelker@uni-goettingen.de."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"267","journalIssueId":4290819,"dateOfPublication":"2027 Jan","monthOfPublication":1,"yearOfPublication":2027,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Theriogenology","title":"Theriogenology","essn":"1879-3231","issn":"0093-691X","nlmid":"0421510","isoabbreviation":"Theriogenology"},"printPublicationDate":"2027-01-01"},"pubYear":"2027","pageInfo":"118215","abstractText":"Despite major progress in generation of edited embryos and offspring via CRISPR/Cas9-RNP electroporation, editing outcomes remain highly variable. This variability may be due to intrinsic differences between in vivo-derived (IVV) and in vitro-derived (IVT) zygotes, which may play a more decisive role than previously anticipated. Therefore, this study aimed to determine how zygote origin shapes the sensitivity to electrical voltage field and editing susceptibility after CRISPR/Cas9-RNP electroporation in the bovine model. The present study systematically compared subsequent developmental competence and editing events in IVV- and IVT-derived bovine zygotes across defined electroporation voltages and pulse durations. Notably, CRISPR/Cas9-RNP electroporation of IVV-derived bovine zygotes of comparable age and collected by tubal embryo flushing (10-18 h post ovulation, hpo) resulted in markedly lower survival rates at 25 and 30 V compared to 20 V. In contrast, higher (p < 0.05) editing frequencies were observed at 25 V and 30 V. Clear differences, however, were observed between the two zygote groups. Following CRISPR/Cas9 RNP electroporation, IVV-derived zygotes yielded a higher proportion of unedited blastocysts (p < 0.05), absence of full edited embryos, a significantly increased frequency of monoallelic edits and reduced rates of mosaicism compared to IVT-derived zygotes. Collectively, the results of the present study clearly underscore a significant lower editing efficiency to pulse sensitivity ratio in IVV-derived zygotes compared to their IVT counterparts. To our knowledge, this is the first study to use bovine IVV-derived zygotes for genome editing using RNP-electroporation to elucidate how zygote origin shapes sensitivity to electrical voltage field and editing efficiency.","affiliation":"University of Goettingen, Department of Animal Science, Biotechnology and Reproduction of Farm Animals, Burckhardtweg 2, Göttingen, 37077, Germany. Electronic address: maryam.rahimi@uni-goettingen.de.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Zygote","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PH","qualifierName":"physiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cattle","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"EM","qualifierName":"embryology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Ribonucleoproteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Electroporation","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Female"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"VE","qualifierName":"veterinary"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Zygote","Editing Susceptibility","Pulse Sensitivity","Rnp-electroporation"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Ribonucleoproteins","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.theriogenology.2026.118215"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-09","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-29","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-09","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-26","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-26","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-30","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42539480","source":"MED","pmid":"42539480","pmcid":"PMC13423668","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13423668"]},"doi":"10.3389/fonc.2026.1888734","title":"CRISPR/Cas9 in cancer therapy: clinical translation, mechanistic strategies, and therapeutic directions.","authorString":"Ng CX, Mustafa S, Yap XY, Lee SH.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Ng CX","lastName":"Ng","firstName":"Chu Xin","initials":"CX","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Mustafa S","lastName":"Mustafa","firstName":"Sakina","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Yap XY","lastName":"Yap","firstName":"Xin Yi","initials":"XY","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}},{"fullName":"Lee SH","lastName":"Lee","firstName":"Sau Har","initials":"SH","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."},{"affiliation":"Digital Health and Medical Advancements Impact Lab, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."},{"affiliation":"Centre for Active Living, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia."}]}}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"16","journalIssueId":4110619,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Oncol","title":"Frontiers in oncology","essn":"2234-943X","issn":"2234-943X","nlmid":"101568867","isoabbreviation":"Front Oncol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1888734","abstractText":"The advent of CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing has significantly transformed the landscape of cancer therapeutics by facilitating precise and programmable manipulation of disease-associated genetic modifications. This review comprehensively evaluates the current clinical and translational landscape of CRISPR/Cas9-based cancer therapies through an analysis of published literature and registered clinical trials. The current CRISPR/Cas9 applications in oncology are primarily centred on three mechanistic strategies: immune cell engineering for enhanced tumor recognition, direct targeting of oncogenic mutations, and modulation of tumor-supportive pathways. Analysis of 32 clinical trials indicates that CRISPR-based interventions have demonstrated encouraging safety profiles and early signs of clinical activity, particularly in <i>ex vivo</i> engineered immune-cell therapies. Notable examples include CRISPR-edited CAR-T cell products targeting <i>CD19</i> and <i>BCMA</i>, which have achieved objective responses in relapsed or refractory hematological malignancies while demonstrating sustained persistence of edited cells <i>in vivo</i>. In contrast, clinical translation into solid tumors remains comparatively limited due to challenges associated with delivery efficiency, tumor heterogeneity, and the immunosuppressive tumor microenvironment. Technological advancements, including multiplex genome editing, base editing, and prime editing have expanded the precision and versatility of CRISPR-based interventions, while integration with immunotherapy and nanotechnology-based delivery systems continues to broaden therapeutic potential. Despite these advances, several significant challenges still need to be addressed, including off-target editing, manufacturing scalability, delivery limitations, and regulatory considerations. Overall, CRISPR/Cas9 represents a promising yet evolving platform in oncology, with its future clinical success dependent on achieving a balance between precision, safety, scalability, and long-term therapeutic durability.","affiliation":"School of Biosciences, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Taylor's University, Selangor, Malaysia.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["review-article","Review","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Cancer therapy","Clinical Trials","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9","Precision Oncology","Car-t Cell Therapy"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fonc.2026.1888734"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13423668"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13423668?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":1,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"Y","tmAccessionTypeList":{"accessionType":["nct","doi"]},"dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-08-13","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstPublicationDate":"2026-07-17","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-03","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-09-04","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42966538","source":"MED","pmid":"42966538","doi":"10.1016/j.biortech.2026.136175","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-directed engineering of Clostridium beijerinckii enhanced butanol production from whey permeate.","authorString":"Okereke O, Duduyemi A, Ujor VC.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Okereke O","lastName":"Okereke","firstName":"Omoaruemike","initials":"O","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Food Science, University of Wisconsin-Madison, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Duduyemi A","lastName":"Duduyemi","firstName":"Ademola","initials":"A","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Food Science, University of Wisconsin-Madison, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Ujor VC","lastName":"Ujor","firstName":"Victor C","initials":"VC","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Food Science, University of Wisconsin-Madison, USA. Electronic address: ujor@wisc.edu."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"465","journalIssueId":4303243,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Bioresour Technol","title":"Bioresource technology","essn":"1873-2976","issn":"0960-8524","nlmid":"9889523","isoabbreviation":"Bioresour Technol"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"136175","abstractText":"Whey permeate (WP), an abundant, lactose-rich byproduct of the dairy industry, constitutes an environmental and economic burden on the industry. Despite the high fermentable sugar content of WP, its bioconversion to biobutanol is limited by poor lactose-utilization culminating in low solvent productivity by native solventogenic microorganisms. In this study, we report CRISPR/Cas9-mediated genome engineering of Clostridium beijerinckii NCIMB 8052 (hereafter, C. beijerinckii) to improve butanol production from lactose-rich WP. A native putative lactose transporter encoded by Cbei_4545 and an aldehyde reductase (mgR) from Clostridium pasteurianum were integrated into the chromosome of C. beijerinckii at the acetoacetate decarboxylase gene locus to improve lactose utilization and solvent production. The resulting recombinant strain was evaluated alongside the wildtype using lactose, untreated WP, and ion exchange (Amberlite™) resin-treated WP. Across all feedstocks tested, the recombinant strain consistently outperformed the wildtype, producing peak butanol titers of 7.9 g/L, 9.7 g/L, and 20.9 g/L on lactose, WP, and resin-treated WP, respectively, compared to 4.3 g/L, 7.1 g/L, and 11.3 g/L for the wildtype. These results demonstrate the utility of targeted CRISPR/Cas9 genome engineering to substantially improve lactose utilization and butanol production in C. beijerinckii. In this work we constructed a strain of C. beijerinckii with strong potential for conversion of abundant and cost-effective WP to biobutanol.","affiliation":"Department of Food Science, University of Wisconsin-Madison, USA.","publicationStatus":"aheadofprint","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"USDA","orderIn":0}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Clostridium Beijerinckii","Waste Valorization","Biobutanol","Whey Permeate","Crispr/cas9 Genome Engineering"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.biortech.2026.136175"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-31","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-02","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-10-31","firstPublicationDate":"2026-10-31","firstIndexDate":"2026-11-01","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42627520","source":"MED","pmid":"42627520","doi":"10.1007/s00299-026-03944-0","title":"Establishment of a CRISPR/Cas9-mediated system for targeted editing of the MFS gene in mint.","authorString":"Lv Z, Wang G.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Lv Z","lastName":"Lv","firstName":"Zhiqin","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Agriculture and Biology, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 800 Dong Chuan Road, Shanghai, 200240, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang G","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Guirong","initials":"G","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Agriculture and Biology, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 800 Dong Chuan Road, Shanghai, 200240, China. grwang@sjtu.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"9","volume":"45","journalIssueId":4239791,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Plant Cell Rep","title":"Plant cell reports","essn":"1432-203X","issn":"0721-7714","nlmid":"9880970","isoabbreviation":"Plant Cell Rep"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"255","abstractText":"<h4>Key message</h4>The key message of this study is that we established a CRISPR/Cas9-mediated genome-editing system for Mentha haplocalyx \"738\" by optimizing protoplast transient assay and screening effective regulatory elements. Targeted knockout of the MFS gene generated edited mint plants with reduced menthofuran content, offering a strategy for quality improvement of mint essential oil. The commercial value of mint (Mentha spp.) essential oil is often diminished by the presence of undesirable metabolites, notably menthofuran, which impairs flavor and raises safety concerns. This study aimed to develop a robust CRISPR/Cas9 gene editing system for mint 738 (Mentha haplocalyx \"738\") and apply it to disrupt the menthofuran synthase (MFS) gene, thereby redirecting metabolic flux to enhance oil quality. We established an optimized system for high-efficiency protoplast isolation and transient transformation from young mint leaves. Key parameters for enzymatic digestion (1.5% cellulase R10, 0.2% macerozyme R-10, 3 h) and PEG-mediated transformation (40% PEG6000, 0.4 M mannitol, 0.4 M CaCl₂) were systematically determined. Using this platform, we screened endogenous regulatory elements, identifying a truncated mint U6 promoter (HmU6.1-3P) and the tomato SlEF1α promoter as the most effective drivers for sgRNA and Cas9 expression, respectively. A CRISPR/Cas9 vector targeting the MFS gene was constructed and used for Agrobacterium-mediated stable transformation. The positive transgenic mint lines were obtained. Sequencing confirmed heritable mutations at the target sites within the MFS gene in multiple independent lines. The results revealed a substantial decrease in menthofuran content in the essential oil of the edited line #10 compared to the wild-type control, thereby demonstrating a viable strategy for improving mint essential oil quality through genome-editing.","affiliation":"School of Agriculture and Biology, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, 800 Dong Chuan Road, Shanghai, 200240, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Contract Research Project","grantId":"23H010204158","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Protoplasts","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plants, Genetically Modified"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mentha","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Oils, Volatile","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plant Proteins","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Transformation, Genetic"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Base Sequence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Protoplast","Menthofuran","Gene Editing","Crispr/cas9","Mint (Mentha)"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Plant Proteins","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Oils, Volatile","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00299-026-03944-0"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-21","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-21","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-21","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-21","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-22","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42819672","source":"MED","pmid":"42819672","pmcid":"PMC13624556","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC13624556"]},"doi":"10.3389/fgeed.2026.1888079","title":"Overcoming delivery barriers of large all-in-one CRISPR/Cas9 plasmids using lysine-based lipid nanoparticles.","authorString":"Sung W, Li T, Takeoka S.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sung W","lastName":"Sung","firstName":"Wenhan","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Life Science and Medical Bioscience, Graduate School of Advanced Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Li T","lastName":"Li","firstName":"Tianshu","initials":"T","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Liberal Arts and Sciences Education Center, Musashi University, Tokyo, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Advanced Research of Biosystem Dynamics, Research Institute for Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}},{"fullName":"Takeoka S","lastName":"Takeoka","firstName":"Shinji","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Life Science and Medical Bioscience, Graduate School of Advanced Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."},{"affiliation":"Institute for Advanced Research of Biosystem Dynamics, Research Institute for Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"volume":"8","journalIssueId":4145489,"dateOfPublication":"2026 ","monthOfPublication":0,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Front Genome Ed","title":"Frontiers in genome editing","essn":"2673-3439","issn":"2673-3439","nlmid":"101775540","isoabbreviation":"Front Genome Ed"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-01-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"1888079","abstractText":"<h4>Introduction</h4>Lipid nanoparticles (LNPs) are widely used as drug delivery systems (DDS) for the intracellular delivery of gene-editing tools such as the clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats (CRISPR)/CRISPR-associated protein 9 (Cas9) system. Previously, a lysine-headgroup cationic lipid (K3C16) was reported to exhibit high biocompatibility and cellular internalization via plasma membrane fusion when formulated as liposomes, suggesting its potential as a low-toxicity gene delivery material. However, the translation of this lipid into a multi-component lipid nanoparticle (LNP) matrix via microfluidic engineering for large cargo encapsulation has not been explored. In this study, K3C16 was successfully engineered for the first time as the main lipid component of LNPs to deliver large plasmid DNA encoding the CRISPR/Cas9 system.<h4>Methods</h4>First, 1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphoethanolamine (DOPE) and 1,2-dioleoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphocholine (DOPC) were evaluated as helper phospholipids to identify an optimal lipid composition. LNPs containing DOPE exhibited significantly higher transfection efficiency than those containing DOPC and were therefore selected for subsequent experiments. Next, LNPs formulated with different cationic or ionizable lipids were prepared and evaluated in HEK293 cells for transfection efficiency, cytotoxicity, and gene-editing efficiency.<h4>Results</h4>LNPs containing the commercial ionizable lipid SM-102 showed higher transfection and gene-editing efficiencies than the lysine-headgroup cationic lipids represent a promising and biocompatible platform for CRISPR/Cas9 plasmid delivery; however, increased cytotoxicity was observed in highly transfected cell populations. In contrast, the lysine-headgroup cationic LMPs achieved effective delivery of large plasmid DNA and CRISPR/Cas9-mediated gene editing while maintaining superior biocompatibility.<h4>Conclusion</h4>These results demonstrate that lysine-headgroup cationic lipids represent a promising and biocompatible platform for CRISPR/Cas9 plasmid delivery. Further optimization of lipid composition and molar ratios may enhance transfection and gene-editing efficiencies, advancing the development of safer nonviral gene delivery systems.","affiliation":"Department of Life Science and Medical Bioscience, Graduate School of Advanced Science and Engineering, Waseda University, Tokyo, Japan.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic-eCollection","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["research-article","Journal Article"]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Biocompatibility","Non-viral Gene Delivery","Cationic Lipids","Crispr/cas9","Lipid Nanoparticles (Lnps)","Aminolipids"]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fgeed.2026.1888079"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13624556"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC13624556?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"Y","license":"cc by","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-10-01","dateOfCreation":"2026-10-01","dateOfRevision":"2026-10-01","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-16","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-16","firstIndexDate":"2026-10-02","fullTextReceivedDate":"2026-10-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42611583","source":"MED","pmid":"42611583","doi":"10.3791/72662","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-Mediated Site-Directed Mutagenesis of Genes in Klebsiella pneumoniae.","authorString":"Chun L, Quan Z, Ke M.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Chun L","lastName":"Chun","firstName":"Liu","initials":"L","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Infectious Diseases, the Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University."}]}},{"fullName":"Quan Z","lastName":"Quan","firstName":"Zhang","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Infectious Diseases, the Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University; Guizhou Provincial Key Laboratory for Digestive System Diseases; State Key Laboratory for Diagnosis and Treatment of Severe Zoonotic Infectious Diseases."}]}},{"fullName":"Ke M","lastName":"Ke","firstName":"Ma","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Infectious Diseases, the Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University; Guizhou Provincial Key Laboratory for Digestive System Diseases; State Key Laboratory for Diagnosis and Treatment of Severe Zoonotic Infectious Diseases; Infection and Hepatology Laboratory, the Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University; ma_ke@gmc.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"234","journalIssueId":4239747,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"J Vis Exp","title":"Journal of visualized experiments : JoVE","essn":"1940-087X","issn":"1940-087X","nlmid":"101313252","isoabbreviation":"J Vis Exp"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-08-01"},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Constructing target-gene mutants with a common genetic background is crucial for elucidating gene function in antimicrobial resistance (AMR) research. Taking advantage of the single-guide RNA (sgRNA) and protospacer adjacent motif (PAM) sequence (3'-NGG) specificity of the Cas9 protein in the CRISPR/Cas9 (Clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats/CRISPR-associated protein 9) system and codon degeneracy, the authors design a repair template that incorporates the desired point mutation while excluding the PAM sequence disrupted by a synonymous substitution, thereby preventing re-cleavage by CRISPR/Cas9. This method employs two available plasmids, pCasKP-apr and pSGKP-spe, offering straightforward operation and high screening specificity. As a result, the approach enables efficient generation of genetically defined mutant strains of Klebsiella pneumoniae (K. pneumoniae) and is readily adaptable to routine laboratory settings. Furthermore, the protocol minimizes off-target editing, shortens experimental timelines, reduces screening workload, and provides a reliable platform for investigating resistance mechanisms, validating candidate genes, and supporting functional genomics studies in clinically relevant bacterial pathogens.","affiliation":"Department of Infectious Diseases, the Affiliated Hospital of Guizhou Medical University.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Video-Audio Media","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Mutagenesis, Site-Directed","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Plasmids","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.3791/72662"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"N","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-08-18","dateOfCreation":"2026-08-18","dateOfRevision":"2026-09-11","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-08-14","firstPublicationDate":"2026-08-14","firstIndexDate":"2026-08-19","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"PPR1249856","source":"PPR","doi":"10.64898/2026.06.08.731005","title":"CRISPR/Cas9 knockout and editing of the major cysteine protease in  <i>Entamoeba histolytica</i>","authorString":"Huang W, Suleiman RL, Luder T, Jebiwott S, Ralston KS.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Huang W","lastName":"Huang","firstName":"Wesley","initials":"W"},{"fullName":"Suleiman RL","lastName":"Suleiman","firstName":"Rene","initials":"RL"},{"fullName":"Luder T","lastName":"Luder","firstName":"Thomas","initials":"T"},{"fullName":"Jebiwott S","lastName":"Jebiwott","firstName":"Sylvia","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Ralston KS","lastName":"Ralston","firstName":"Katherine","initials":"KS","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1604-7634"}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-1604-7634"}]},"pubYear":"2026","abstractText":"Entamoeba histolytica is the cause of amoebiasis, a diarrheal infection that is a significant source of morbidity and mortality. Despite its importance to human health, the basic mechanism of disease is poorly understood. E. histolytica is dramatically understudied, even in comparison to other understudied parasites, due to challenging features of its genome and the relative paucity of genetic tools. A major limitation is the historical lack of gene knockouts or manipulation of the endogenous genome. While CRISPR/Cas9 has led to major advances in the tractability of numerous parasites, until recently, it was not applied in E. histolytica, except for the demonstration of editing of an episomal plasmid. In this study, we further developed CRISPR/Cas9 and demonstrated the successful targeting of the endogenous cysteine protease 5 (CP5) gene, a major virulence factor. Further, we demonstrated successful CRISPR/Cas9-based editing of this locus, which represents the very first successful editing of the endogenous genome. In the course of these approaches, we developed the viral skip peptide approach as well as a suite of endogenous promoters to drive tunable levels of gene expression. We also performed the most extensive empirical testing of nuclear localization signals in E. histolytica that has been done. Thus, this work demonstrates effective CRISPR/Cas9-based manipulation of the endogenous genome, together with significant advances to the overall genetic toolkit that extend beyond CRISPR/Cas9. These great improvements in the tractability of E. histolytica thus set the stage for future studies to better define its virulence.","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Preprint"]},"bookOrReportDetails":{"publisher":"bioRxiv","yearOfPublication":2026},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"1R01AI189918","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Institutes of Health","grantId":"1R01AI146914","orderIn":0}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Free","availabilityCode":"F","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.06.08.731005"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCreation":"2026-06-11","firstPublicationDate":"2026-06-09","firstIndexDate":"2026-06-11","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42684483","source":"MED","pmid":"42684483","doi":"10.1007/s10126-026-10702-6","title":"Efficient Microinjection and CRISPR/Cas9 Mediated Genome Editing in Urechis unicinctus.","authorString":"Wang K, Chen J, Bai S, Wang Q, Zhang W, Qin Z, Zhang Z, Zhang Z, Ma Y.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Wang K","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Kai","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Chen J","lastName":"Chen","firstName":"Jiao","initials":"J","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Bai S","lastName":"Bai","firstName":"Shumiao","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Wang Q","lastName":"Wang","firstName":"Qing","initials":"Q","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang W","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Wei","initials":"W","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Qin Z","lastName":"Qin","firstName":"Zhenkui","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Z","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Zhengrui","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Zhang Z","lastName":"Zhang","firstName":"Zhifeng","initials":"Z","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China."},{"affiliation":"Key Laboratory of Tropical Aquatic Germplasm of Hainan Province, Sanya Ocean Institute, Ocean University of China, Sanya, 572000, China."}]}},{"fullName":"Ma Y","lastName":"Ma","firstName":"Yubin","initials":"Y","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China. mayubin@ouc.edu.cn."},{"affiliation":"Shandong Key Laboratory of Marine Seed Industry (preparatory), Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China. mayubin@ouc.edu.cn."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"5","volume":"28","journalIssueId":4252058,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Sep","monthOfPublication":9,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Mar Biotechnol (NY)","title":"Marine biotechnology (New York, N.Y.)","essn":"1436-2236","issn":"1436-2228","nlmid":"100892712","isoabbreviation":"Mar Biotechnol (NY)"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-09-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"147","abstractText":"Urechis unicinctus is an economically important aquaculture species and an emerging model for developmental and evolutionary research; however, progress in functional genetic studies in this species has remained limited by the absence of effective genome editing approaches. Here, we establish a CRISPR/Cas9-mediated genome editing platform in U. unicinctus based on an optimized microinjection procedure. Targeting the cilia-associated gene Caveolin-1, Cas9/sgRNA ribonucleoprotein complexes were delivered into oocytes, generating mosaic G0 larvae harboring insertion-deletion mutations, as confirmed by ICE analysis and cloning-based Sanger sequencing with mutation detection rates of 68.51% and 64.86%, respectively. Ciliary defects were frequently observed in edited larvae, including shortening of the circumoral ciliary ring and reduction of apical ciliary tufts, accompanied by impaired swimming performance. Whole-mount in situ hybridization further revealed altered spatial expression patterns of Caveolin-1 in edited embryos, with 78% of individuals showing detectable changes in expression patterns. To independently assess the robustness of the platform, the conserved cytoskeletal gene α-tubulin was additionally targeted, resulting in successful mutagenesis and associated ciliary abnormalities. Together, these findings establish the first CRISPR/Cas9 genome editing workflow for U. unicinctus, providing a technical framework for functional genomics in echiurans and facilitating future studies of developmental mechanisms and molecular breeding in this species.","affiliation":"Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of Marine Genetics and Breeding, College of Marine Life Sciences, Ocean University of China, Qingdao, 266003, China.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities","grantId":"2022120090","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"32170373","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Natural Science Foundation of Shandong Province","grantId":"ZR2025MS368","orderIn":0},{"agency":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grantId":"42176122","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cilia","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Tubulin","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Microinjections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Base Sequence"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Caveolin 1","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifierName":"metabolism"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Urechis unicinctus","Caveolin-1","Genome Editing","Crispr/cas9"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"Tubulin","registryNumber":"0"},{"name":"Caveolin 1","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10126-026-10702-6"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2026-09-02","dateOfCreation":"2026-09-02","dateOfRevision":"2026-11-05","electronicPublicationDate":"2026-09-02","firstPublicationDate":"2026-09-02","firstIndexDate":"2026-09-03","hasEvaluations":"N"},{"id":"42862306","source":"MED","pmid":"42862306","doi":"10.1007/s00011-026-02383-7","title":"CRISPR/Cas9-based nanodelivery systems for precision gene editing in neuroinflammation.","authorString":"Sahu S, Jain P, Dharme KK, Yadav K.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sahu S","lastName":"Sahu","firstName":"Sanjana","initials":"S","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Rungta College of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research, Rungta International Skills University, Kohka, Bhilai, 490024, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Jain P","lastName":"Jain","firstName":"Parag","initials":"P","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Rungta College of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research, Rungta International Skills University, Kohka, Bhilai, 490024, India. paragjain1510@gmail.com."}]}},{"fullName":"Dharme KK","lastName":"Dharme","firstName":"Karan Kumar","initials":"KK","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Rungta College of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research, Rungta International Skills University, Kohka, Bhilai, 490024, India."}]}},{"fullName":"Yadav K","lastName":"Yadav","firstName":"Krishna","initials":"K","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Rungta College of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research, Rungta International Skills University, Kohka, Bhilai, 490024, India."}]}}]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"1","volume":"75","journalIssueId":4285761,"dateOfPublication":"2026 Oct","monthOfPublication":10,"yearOfPublication":2026,"journal":{"medlineAbbreviation":"Inflamm Res","title":"Inflammation research : official journal of the European Histamine Research Society ... [et al.]","essn":"1420-908X","issn":"1023-3830","nlmid":"9508160","isoabbreviation":"Inflamm Res"},"printPublicationDate":"2026-10-01"},"pubYear":"2026","pageInfo":"237","abstractText":"<h4>Background</h4>Neuroinflammation is a characteristic feature of many neurological diseases, such as Alzheimer's disease, Parkinson's disease, Huntington's disease, and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. Uncontrolled activation of microglia and astrocytes results in the production of proinflammatory mediators that contribute to neuronal dysfunction and degeneration.<h4>Purpose</h4>CRISPR/Cas9 genome-editing technology provides highly precise targeting of genes involved in these pathways, enabling the deletion, correction, or modulation of disease-associated genetic alterations. Nonetheless, effective delivery of CRISPR components to the central nervous system (CNS) remains a major challenge because of protective barriers such as the blood-brain barrier (BBB), as well as concerns regarding genomic instability and immunogenicity.<h4>Methods</h4>Nanotechnology-guided delivery platforms, such as lipid nanoparticles, liposomes, dendrimers and polymeric carriers, have been developed as promising strategies to address these challenges. These nanosystems can improve biodistribution, pharmacokinetic behavior, and cellular uptake, while potentially reducing off-target effects and systemic toxicity.<h4>Results</h4>The preclinical studies demonstrate successful modulation of key neuroinflammatory targets, including the NLRP3 inflammasome, triggering receptor expressed on myeloid cells 2 (TREM2), Janus kinase (JAK) signaling, and the cyclic GMP-AMP synthase-stimulator of interferon genes (cGAS-STING) pathway, with potential therapeutic relevance to neurodegenerative diseases. In addition, novel techniques, such as mitochondrial base editing, prime editing, and epigenetic modulation, expand the scope of CRISPR-mediated therapeutic strategies. Although outcomes from animal models are promising, translation to the clinical setting requires careful consideration of safety, ethical concerns, and long-term efficacy.<h4>Conclusions</h4>This review highlights the potential of integrating CRISPR/Cas9 with nanodelivery systems to advance precision medicine for neuroinflammatory disorders, while emphasizing their potential for therapeutic applications in complex CNS conditions. Ongoing studies aimed at optimizing delivery and reducing off-target effects will be essential for advancing these approaches toward clinical practice.","affiliation":"School of Pharmacy, Rungta College of Pharmaceutical Sciences and Research, Rungta International Skills University, Kohka, Bhilai, 490024, India.","publicationStatus":"epublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Review","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"Probecell Scientific Pvt. Ltd.","grantId":"Probecell/Res/2026/124","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Animals"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nanoparticles","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"AD","qualifierName":"administration & dosage"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"CRISPR-Cas Systems"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Gene Editing","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MT","qualifierName":"methods"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Neuroinflammatory Diseases","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"GE","qualifierName":"genetics"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"TH","qualifierName":"therapy"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["BBB","neuroinflammation","Nlrp3","Crispr/cas9","Nanodelivery","Jak Signaling"]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00011-026-02383-7"}]},"isOpenAccess":"N","inEPMC":"N","inPMC":"N","citedByCount":0,"hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasPDF":"N","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"N","hasLabsLinks":"N","hasData":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuth